(window.CONTENT_PARTS = window.CONTENT_PARTS || {})["notes-primary"] = {"acid-base":"<h2 id=\"definitions\">Definitions</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>pH = −log<sub>10</sub>[H<sup>+</sup>].</strong> Normal arterial pH 7.35–7.45, i.e. [H<sup>+</sup>] <strong>35–45 nmol/L</strong> (pH 7.4 = 40 nmol/L). A strong acid dissociates completely; a weak acid partially.</li><li><strong>Buffers</strong> resist changes in pH and work best within about one pH unit of their <strong>pKa</strong>.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-bicarbonate-buffer\">The bicarbonate buffer</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Henderson–Hasselbalch:</strong> <strong>pH = 6.1 + log<sub>10</sub>([HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup>] / (0.23 × PCO<sub>2</sub> in kPa))</strong> (0.03 × PCO<sub>2</sub> if in mmHg).</li><li>Although its pKa (6.1) is far from 7.4, it is the most important extracellular buffer because it is an <strong>open system</strong>: CO<sub>2</sub> is regulated by the lungs and HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> by the kidneys.</li><li>Other buffers: <strong>haemoglobin</strong> (histidine imidazole groups; the most important non-bicarbonate buffer in blood), plasma proteins, phosphate (important intracellularly and in urine), bone.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"derived-variables\">Derived variables</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Standard bicarbonate:</strong> HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> after equilibration at PCO<sub>2</sub> 5.3 kPa and 37°C: removes the respiratory component.</li><li><strong>Base excess:</strong> the amount of strong acid (mmol/L) needed to return blood to pH 7.4 at PCO<sub>2</sub> 5.3 kPa and 37°C. Normal −2 to +2; negative values indicate metabolic acidosis. <strong>Standard base excess</strong> is calculated for extracellular fluid (Hb about 50 g/L) and is more representative of the whole body.</li><li><strong>Anion gap = (Na<sup>+</sup> + K<sup>+</sup>) − (Cl<sup>−</sup> + HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup>)</strong>, normally about 12–16 mmol/L with K<sup>+</sup> (8–12 without). Unmeasured anions are mostly <strong>albumin</strong>: correct for low albumin (add about 2.5 mmol/L per 10 g/L fall).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"four-primary-disorders-and-their-compensation\">Four primary disorders and their compensation</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Disorder</th><th>Primary change</th><th>Compensation</th><th>Expected compensation (rules of thumb)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Metabolic acidosis</td><td>↓ HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup></td><td>Hyperventilation ↓ PCO<sub>2</sub></td><td>PaCO<sub>2</sub> (mmHg) ≈ 1.5 × HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> + 8 (± 2) (Winter's formula)</td></tr><tr><td>Metabolic alkalosis</td><td>↑ HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup></td><td>Hypoventilation ↑ PCO<sub>2</sub> (limited by hypoxia)</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Respiratory acidosis</td><td>↑ PCO<sub>2</sub></td><td>Renal HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> retention (days)</td><td>Acute: HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> ↑ ~1 per 10 mmHg; chronic: ~3.5–4 per 10 mmHg</td></tr><tr><td>Respiratory alkalosis</td><td>↓ PCO<sub>2</sub></td><td>Renal HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> excretion</td><td>Acute: ↓ ~2 per 10 mmHg; chronic: ↓ ~4–5 per 10 mmHg</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Compensation <strong>does not overcorrect</strong>; if the pH is on the \"other side\", suspect a mixed disorder.</p>\n<h2 id=\"metabolic-acidosis-the-anion-gap\">Metabolic acidosis: the anion gap</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Raised gap:</strong> lactate, ketones (diabetic, alcoholic, starvation), renal failure (sulphate, phosphate), toxins (methanol, ethylene glycol, salicylate).</li><li><strong>Normal gap (hyperchloraemic):</strong> large volumes of 0.9% saline, GI bicarbonate loss (diarrhoea, fistulae, ileal conduit), renal tubular acidosis, acetazolamide.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-stewart-physicochemical-approach-in-brief\">The Stewart (physicochemical) approach in brief</h2>\n<p>pH is determined by three independent variables: <strong>PCO<sub>2</sub></strong>, the <strong>strong ion difference</strong> (SID ≈ Na<sup>+</sup> + K<sup>+</sup> − Cl<sup>−</sup> − lactate; normal about 40 mEq/L) and <strong>total weak acids</strong> (mainly albumin and phosphate). Bicarbonate is a dependent variable. It explains why 0.9% saline (SID 0) causes acidosis and why hypoalbuminaemia causes alkalosis.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> Henderson–Hasselbalch calculations; base excess; anion gap with albumin correction; expected compensation.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> interpret a blood gas from a septic patient step by step, and explain what you would do about it.</li></ul></div>","ageing-obesity":"<h2 id=\"ageing\">Ageing</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>System</th><th>Change</th><th>Implication</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cardiovascular</td><td>Stiffer arteries (↑ systolic BP, wide pulse pressure), diastolic dysfunction, reduced β-receptor responsiveness, conduction disease</td><td>Exaggerated hypotension at induction; poor tolerance of tachycardia and fluid overload</td></tr><tr><td>Respiratory</td><td>↑ closing capacity, ↓ compliance of chest wall, ↓ PaO<sub>2</sub> (a rule of thumb: about 100 − age/3 mmHg breathing air), blunted responses to hypoxia and hypercapnia</td><td>Hypoxaemia; sensitivity to respiratory depressants</td></tr><tr><td>Renal</td><td>↓ GFR (about 1 mL/min per year after 40), ↓ concentrating ability</td><td>Reduced drug clearance; creatinine underestimates impairment (low muscle mass)</td></tr><tr><td>CNS</td><td>↓ neurones and neurotransmitters, <strong>↓ MAC (about 6% per decade after 40)</strong></td><td>Postoperative delirium and cognitive dysfunction</td></tr><tr><td>Body composition</td><td>↑ fat, ↓ muscle and total body water</td><td>Larger Vd for lipid-soluble drugs, smaller for water-soluble ones</td></tr><tr><td>Other</td><td>Thinner skin, osteoporosis, impaired thermoregulation, polypharmacy</td><td>Positioning injury, hypothermia, drug interactions</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Frailty</strong> (reduced physiological reserve, often measured with the Clinical Frailty Scale) predicts complications and mortality better than age alone.</p>\n<h2 id=\"obesity\">Obesity</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>BMI = weight (kg) / height (m)<sup>2</sup>.</strong> Obese ≥ 30; class III ≥ 40.</li><li><strong>Respiratory:</strong> ↓ FRC and ERV (especially supine), atelectasis, ↑ oxygen consumption and CO<sub>2</sub> production, ↑ work of breathing, <strong>obstructive sleep apnoea</strong> (screen with STOP-BANG) and obesity hypoventilation syndrome. Rapid desaturation; potential difficult mask ventilation.</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular:</strong> ↑ blood volume and cardiac output, hypertension, LV hypertrophy, pulmonary hypertension (OSA), arrhythmias.</li><li><strong>Other:</strong> diabetes, reflux, fatty liver, VTE risk, difficult venous access, pressure injuries.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"size-scalars-for-drug-dosing\">Size scalars for drug dosing</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Ideal body weight (IBW)</strong> (Devine): men 50 kg + 0.9 kg/cm above 152 cm; women 45.5 kg + 0.9 kg/cm above 152 cm. A simple alternative: height (cm) − 100 (men) or − 105 (women).</li><li><strong>Lean body weight (LBW)</strong>: fat-free mass; formulae (e.g. Janmahasatian) plateau at high BMI.</li><li><strong>Adjusted body weight</strong> = IBW + 0.4 × (actual − IBW).</li><li>General principles: <strong>induction agents and opioids</strong> dosed on <strong>LBW</strong> (then titrated); <strong>suxamethonium</strong> on <strong>total body weight</strong> (increased plasma cholinesterase and ECF); <strong>non-depolarising NMBs</strong> on <strong>IBW</strong> (or LBW); <strong>sugammadex</strong> on actual or adjusted weight per product guidance; LMWH per local obesity protocols. TCI models must be used within their validated ranges.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> changes in closing capacity and MAC with age; weight scalars and which drugs use which.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> planning induction for a patient with a BMI of 48 and OSA: position, pre-oxygenation, drug doses and extubation.</li></ul></div>","blood-haemostasis":"<h2 id=\"primary-haemostasis\">Primary haemostasis</h2>\n<p>Vessel injury → vasoconstriction → <strong>platelet adhesion</strong> to exposed collagen via <strong>von Willebrand factor</strong> (binding platelet <strong>GPIb</strong>) → <strong>activation</strong> (shape change; release of ADP, thromboxane A<sub>2</sub>, serotonin) → <strong>aggregation</strong> via <strong>GPIIb/IIIa</strong> receptors cross-linked by fibrinogen. Normal platelet count 150–400 × 10<sup>9</sup>/L; lifespan 7–10 days.</p>\n<h2 id=\"coagulation-the-cell-based-model\">Coagulation: the cell-based model</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Initiation:</strong> <strong>tissue factor</strong> (TF) on subendothelial cells binds <strong>factor VIIa</strong>, activating small amounts of IX and X → a little thrombin.</li><li><strong>Amplification:</strong> thrombin activates platelets and factors <strong>V, VIII and XI</strong>.</li><li><strong>Propagation:</strong> on the activated platelet surface, the <strong>tenase (IXa–VIIIa)</strong> and <strong>prothrombinase (Xa–Va)</strong> complexes generate a burst of thrombin, which converts <strong>fibrinogen to fibrin</strong>; <strong>factor XIIIa</strong> cross-links it.</li><li>The classical <strong>extrinsic</strong> (TF, VII) and <strong>intrinsic</strong> (XII, XI, IX, VIII) pathways remain useful for interpreting laboratory tests.</li><li><strong>Vitamin K-dependent factors:</strong> II, VII, IX, X (and proteins C and S). Calcium is required for most steps (citrate anticoagulates stored blood).</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"natural-anticoagulants-and-fibrinolysis\">Natural anticoagulants and fibrinolysis</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Antithrombin</strong> inactivates thrombin and Xa (accelerated about 1000-fold by heparin). <strong>Protein C</strong> (activated by thrombin–thrombomodulin) with <strong>protein S</strong> inactivates Va and VIIIa. <strong>Tissue factor pathway inhibitor</strong> limits initiation.</li><li><strong>Fibrinolysis:</strong> <strong>tPA</strong> converts plasminogen to <strong>plasmin</strong>, which breaks down fibrin into fibrin degradation products (<strong>D-dimers</strong>). <strong>Tranexamic acid</strong> blocks lysine-binding sites on plasminogen.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"tests\">Tests</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Test</th><th>Measures</th><th>Prolonged by</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>PT / INR</strong></td><td>Extrinsic and common (VII, X, V, II, fibrinogen)</td><td>Warfarin, liver disease, vitamin K deficiency, DIC</td></tr><tr><td><strong>APTT</strong></td><td>Intrinsic and common (XII, XI, IX, VIII, X, V, II)</td><td>Unfractionated heparin, haemophilia, lupus anticoagulant (in vitro)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Fibrinogen (Clauss)</strong></td><td>Normal ~2–4 g/L; ≥ 2 g/L is a common target in obstetric haemorrhage</td><td>Dilution, consumption (DIC, obstetric haemorrhage)</td></tr><tr><td>Anti-Xa assay</td><td>Heparin effect (LMWH, UFH) and some DOACs</td><td></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Viscoelastic tests (TEG, ROTEM)</strong></td><td>Whole-blood clot formation, strength and lysis</td><td>Guide targeted treatment in major haemorrhage</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li>Viscoelastic tests show the <strong>time to start clotting</strong> (factor deficiency), <strong>clot strength</strong> (fibrinogen and platelets) and <strong>lysis</strong> (hyperfibrinolysis), within minutes. Their use in obstetric haemorrhage is supported by a national consensus.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"common-disorders\">Common disorders</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Haemophilia A (VIII) and B (IX):</strong> X-linked; prolonged APTT; factor replacement before surgery with haematology input.</li><li><strong>Von Willebrand disease:</strong> the commonest inherited bleeding disorder; mucosal bleeding; <strong>desmopressin</strong> releases vWF in type 1.</li><li><strong>DIC:</strong> widespread activation with consumption of factors and platelets and secondary fibrinolysis (sepsis, obstetric emergencies, trauma).</li><li><strong>Liver disease:</strong> reduced synthesis of both pro- and anticoagulant factors (\"rebalanced\" haemostasis): INR overstates bleeding risk.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> vitamin K-dependent factors; what PT and APTT measure; interpreting a viscoelastic trace.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> a bleeding patient after major surgery with a coagulation screen and ROTEM result: interpret and prioritise treatment.</li></ul></div>","blood-rbc-hb":"<h2 id=\"red-cells\">Red cells</h2>\n<ul><li>Produced in bone marrow under the control of <strong>erythropoietin</strong> (from peritubular cells of the kidney, released in response to hypoxia); lifespan about <strong>120 days</strong>; removed by the spleen.</li><li>No nucleus or mitochondria: energy from <strong>anaerobic glycolysis</strong> (Embden–Meyerhof). The <strong>Rapoport–Luebering shunt</strong> produces <strong>2,3-DPG</strong>; the <strong>pentose phosphate pathway</strong> produces NADPH to protect against oxidative damage (<strong>G6PD deficiency</strong> → haemolysis with oxidant drugs and infection). <strong>Methaemoglobin reductase</strong> keeps iron in the ferrous (Fe<sup>2+</sup>) state.</li><li>Haematinics: <strong>iron, vitamin B<sub>12</sub> and folate</strong> (B<sub>12</sub> and folate deficiency → macrocytic anaemia; nitrous oxide inactivates B<sub>12</sub>-dependent methionine synthase).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"haemoglobin\">Haemoglobin</h2>\n<ul><li>Tetramer of four globin chains, each with a haem group (protoporphyrin + <strong>Fe<sup>2+</sup></strong>) binding one O<sub>2</sub>. Adult <strong>HbA = α<sub>2</sub>β<sub>2</sub></strong> (~97%), HbA<sub>2</sub> = α<sub>2</sub>δ<sub>2</sub>, <strong>HbF = α<sub>2</sub>γ<sub>2</sub></strong> (higher affinity; P50 ~2.5 kPa).</li><li><strong>Sickle haemoglobin (HbS):</strong> a β-chain glutamate → valine substitution; polymerises when deoxygenated → sickling, vaso-occlusion and haemolysis. Avoid hypoxia, dehydration, hypothermia, acidosis and stasis; sickle cell disease patients need specialist perioperative planning, often including transfusion.</li><li><strong>Thalassaemias:</strong> reduced synthesis of α or β chains.</li><li><strong>Methaemoglobin (Fe<sup>3+</sup>)</strong> cannot carry oxygen; causes include nitrates, prilocaine (o-toluidine), dapsone. Pulse oximetry reads around 85% whatever the true saturation. Treat with <strong>methylthioninium chloride (methylene blue)</strong> unless G6PD deficient.</li><li><strong>Carboxyhaemoglobin:</strong> see oxygen transport.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"iron\">Iron</h2>\n<ul><li>Absorbed in the <strong>duodenum</strong> as Fe<sup>2+</sup> (enhanced by acid and vitamin C); regulated by <strong>hepcidin</strong>, which rises in inflammation and blocks absorption and release (the anaemia of chronic disease). Transported by <strong>transferrin</strong>, stored as <strong>ferritin</strong> (an acute phase protein; low ferritin is diagnostic of iron deficiency, but normal or high ferritin does not exclude it when inflammation is present: check transferrin saturation).</li><li><strong>Preoperative iron deficiency anaemia</strong> should be treated (often with IV iron when time is short), as part of patient blood management.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"anaemia\">Anaemia</h2>\n<ul><li>WHO definition: Hb &lt; 130 g/L in men and &lt; 120 g/L in non-pregnant women.</li><li><strong>Acute adaptations:</strong> increased cardiac output (lower viscosity, sympathetic activity), increased oxygen extraction. <strong>Chronic:</strong> raised 2,3-DPG (right shift), increased plasma volume.</li><li>Perioperative anaemia is associated with worse outcomes and more transfusion; restrictive transfusion thresholds (e.g. 70 g/L, or 80 g/L with cardiovascular disease) are generally recommended.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"polycythaemia\">Polycythaemia</h2>\n<p>Primary (polycythaemia vera) or secondary (hypoxia, altitude, smoking, EPO-secreting tumours). Raised viscosity increases thrombotic risk; in polycythaemia vera, venesection before elective surgery reduces complications.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> red cell metabolism and G6PD; Hb variants and their P50; causes of methaemoglobinaemia; iron studies.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> a patient listed for major surgery with a Hb of 98 g/L: what investigations and optimisation would you arrange?</li></ul></div>","blood-transfusion":"<h2 id=\"blood-groups\">Blood groups</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Antigens on red cells</th><th>Antibodies in plasma</th><th>UK frequency (approx.)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>O</td><td>None</td><td>Anti-A and anti-B</td><td>~45%</td></tr><tr><td>A</td><td>A</td><td>Anti-B</td><td>~40%</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>B</td><td>Anti-A</td><td>~10%</td></tr><tr><td>AB</td><td>A and B</td><td>None</td><td>~5%</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li>ABO antibodies are <strong>naturally occurring IgM</strong>, which fix complement: ABO-incompatible transfusion causes <strong>intravascular haemolysis</strong> and can be fatal.</li><li><strong>Group O</strong> red cells are the universal donor for red cells (O RhD-negative in emergencies for women of childbearing potential and unknown group); <strong>AB plasma</strong> is the universal plasma donor.</li><li><strong>Rhesus D:</strong> about 85% RhD-positive. Anti-D is IgG and develops only after exposure (transfusion or pregnancy); it can cross the placenta → <strong>haemolytic disease of the fetus and newborn</strong>. Anti-D immunoglobulin prevents sensitisation.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"compatibility-testing\">Compatibility testing</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Group and screen:</strong> determines ABO and RhD group and screens the plasma for irregular antibodies.</li><li><strong>Crossmatch:</strong> serological (patient plasma against donor cells) or <strong>electronic issue</strong> when the screen is negative and valid samples exist. Emergency O-negative is available immediately; group-specific blood within about 10–15 minutes.</li><li><strong>Two independent samples</strong> (or a second check) are usually needed before group-specific blood is issued, to prevent wrong-blood-in-tube errors, which are a major cause of ABO incompatibility.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"components\">Components</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Storage</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Red cells</td><td>2–6°C, up to 35 days</td><td>Hct ~0.5–0.7 in SAG-M; one unit raises Hb by about 10 g/L in an adult</td></tr><tr><td>Platelets</td><td>20–24°C with agitation, up to 7 days</td><td>Highest risk of bacterial contamination</td></tr><tr><td>Fresh frozen plasma</td><td>−25°C or below</td><td>Contains all coagulation factors; 12–15 mL/kg</td></tr><tr><td>Cryoprecipitate</td><td>Frozen</td><td>Rich in fibrinogen, VIII, vWF, XIII</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Changes in stored red cells: rising K<sup>+</sup>, falling pH and 2,3-DPG, microaggregates, cold. Citrate binds calcium.</p>\n<h2 id=\"transfusion-reactions-and-complications\">Transfusion reactions and complications</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Acute haemolytic (ABO incompatible):</strong> fever, hypotension, loin pain, haemoglobinuria, DIC; under anaesthesia may present only as hypotension, tachycardia and bleeding. <strong>Stop the transfusion</strong>, keep the line, resuscitate, check the patient ID against the unit, inform the lab, send samples.</li><li><strong>Febrile non-haemolytic reactions</strong> (cytokines), <strong>allergic reactions</strong> (urticaria) up to <strong>anaphylaxis</strong> (IgA-deficient patients with anti-IgA).</li><li><strong>TACO</strong> (transfusion-associated circulatory overload): the commonest serious cause of transfusion-related morbidity; elderly and cardiac patients; hypertension, pulmonary oedema.</li><li><strong>TRALI:</strong> acute lung injury within 6 hours, from donor antibodies against recipient leucocytes.</li><li><strong>Bacterial contamination</strong> (platelets), <strong>delayed haemolytic reaction</strong> (days to weeks), <strong>transfusion-associated graft-versus-host disease</strong> (prevented by irradiation in at-risk patients).</li><li><strong>Massive transfusion:</strong> hypothermia, hypocalcaemia, hyperkalaemia, dilutional coagulopathy, acidosis.</li><li>Serious hazards are reported to the UK haemovigilance scheme (SHOT) and MHRA (SABRE).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> ABO antibodies and their type; storage of components; features of TRALI versus TACO.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> \"The patient becomes hypotensive 10 minutes after the second unit of blood is started under GA\": run the emergency and explain it to the consultant.</li></ul></div>","cell-physiology":"<h2 id=\"cells-and-homeostasis\">Cells and homeostasis</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Homeostasis</strong> (Claude Bernard's <em>milieu intérieur</em>; Cannon): the maintenance of a stable internal environment by negative feedback (sensor → integrator → effector).</li><li><strong>Organelles:</strong> nucleus (DNA; transcription to mRNA), ribosomes and rough ER (protein synthesis; translation), smooth ER (lipid synthesis, drug metabolism: the \"microsomal\" enzymes), Golgi (packaging), mitochondria (oxidative phosphorylation; own DNA, inherited maternally), lysosomes, peroxisomes, cytoskeleton.</li><li><strong>Gene expression:</strong> DNA → (transcription) → mRNA → (translation) → protein, regulated by transcription factors; steroid and thyroid hormones act here.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-cell-membrane\">The cell membrane</h2>\n<ul><li>A <strong>phospholipid bilayer</strong> with cholesterol and proteins (the fluid mosaic model): lipid-soluble substances (O<sub>2</sub>, CO<sub>2</sub>, anaesthetic agents) cross easily; ions and polar molecules need channels or carriers.</li><li>Cell junctions: tight junctions (barriers such as the blood–brain barrier), gap junctions (electrical coupling in cardiac and smooth muscle), desmosomes (mechanical strength).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"transport-across-membranes\">Transport across membranes</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Mechanism</th><th>Energy</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Simple diffusion</td><td>None; down the gradient (Fick's law)</td><td>O<sub>2</sub>, CO<sub>2</sub>, lipid-soluble drugs</td></tr><tr><td>Facilitated diffusion</td><td>None; carrier or channel; saturable</td><td>Glucose via GLUT transporters</td></tr><tr><td>Primary active transport</td><td>ATP directly</td><td><strong>Na<sup>+</sup>/K<sup>+</sup>-ATPase</strong>, H<sup>+</sup>/K<sup>+</sup>-ATPase, Ca<sup>2+</sup>-ATPase, P-glycoprotein</td></tr><tr><td>Secondary active transport</td><td>Uses a gradient set up by primary transport</td><td>SGLT (Na<sup>+</sup>–glucose), Na<sup>+</sup>/H<sup>+</sup> exchange, Na<sup>+</sup>/Ca<sup>2+</sup> exchange</td></tr><tr><td>Endocytosis / exocytosis</td><td>ATP</td><td>Neurotransmitter release</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li><strong>Fick's law:</strong> rate of diffusion ∝ (area × concentration gradient × solubility) / (thickness × √molecular weight) — the square-root term is Graham's law.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"enzymes\">Enzymes</h2>\n<ul><li>Biological catalysts that lower activation energy; activity depends on temperature, pH and cofactors.</li><li><strong>Michaelis–Menten:</strong> rate = V<sub>max</sub>[S] / (K<sub>m</sub> + [S]). K<sub>m</sub> is the substrate concentration at half V<sub>max</sub>. At low [S] the rate is proportional to [S] (<strong>first-order</strong>); when saturated it is constant (<strong>zero-order</strong>), e.g. ethanol, phenytoin and high-dose aspirin.</li><li>Inhibition: competitive (raises apparent K<sub>m</sub>), non-competitive (lowers V<sub>max</sub>), irreversible (organophosphates, aspirin on COX).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"protective-mechanisms\">Protective mechanisms</h2>\n<p>Skin and mucosal barriers, mucociliary clearance, gastric acid, inflammation, the immune system, coagulation, and pain as a warning signal.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> transport types with examples; Fick's law; Michaelis–Menten and zero-order kinetics.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> explaining why phenytoin levels can rise disproportionately with a small dose increase.</li></ul></div>","cvs-bp-control":"<h2 id=\"the-basics\">The basics</h2>\n<p><strong>MAP ≈ CO × SVR</strong> (strictly, MAP − CVP = CO × SVR). MAP ≈ diastolic + ⅓ pulse pressure at normal heart rates.</p>\n<h2 id=\"short-term-the-baroreceptor-reflex-seconds\">Short-term: the baroreceptor reflex (seconds)</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>High-pressure baroreceptors</strong> in the <strong>carotid sinus</strong> (glossopharyngeal nerve, IX) and <strong>aortic arch</strong> (vagus, X) fire in proportion to stretch.</li><li>Signals go to the <strong>nucleus tractus solitarius</strong>; a fall in pressure reduces firing → less vagal (parasympathetic) output and more sympathetic output → tachycardia, increased contractility, vasoconstriction and venoconstriction.</li><li>The reflex <strong>resets</strong> within days in chronic hypertension. It is blunted by volatile agents, propofol, age, diabetic autonomic neuropathy and β-blockers.</li><li><strong>Low-pressure receptors</strong> in the atria and great veins respond to volume: stretch releases <strong>ANP</strong> and reduces ADH and sympathetic output. The <strong>Bainbridge reflex</strong> is a rise in heart rate with atrial stretch.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"medium-and-long-term-minutes-to-days\">Medium and long term (minutes to days)</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Renin–angiotensin–aldosterone system:</strong> renal hypoperfusion, sympathetic β<sub>1</sub> stimulation and low distal tubular sodium release renin → angiotensin II (vasoconstriction, thirst, ADH and aldosterone release) → sodium and water retention.</li><li><strong>ADH (vasopressin):</strong> released for raised osmolality and (at larger volume losses) hypovolaemia; V<sub>1</sub> receptors cause vasoconstriction, V<sub>2</sub> receptors retain water.</li><li><strong>Pressure natriuresis:</strong> the kidney's long-term set point for pressure.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-valsalva-manoeuvre-forced-expiration-against-a-closed-glottis-40-mmhg\">The Valsalva manoeuvre (forced expiration against a closed glottis, ~40 mmHg)</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Phase</th><th>Blood pressure</th><th>Heart rate</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>I (onset)</td><td>Brief <strong>rise</strong> (intrathoracic pressure squeezes blood out of the aorta)</td><td>Reflex slight fall</td></tr><tr><td>II (strain)</td><td><strong>Falls</strong> as venous return drops, then partly recovers with sympathetic vasoconstriction</td><td><strong>Rises</strong></td></tr><tr><td>III (release)</td><td>Brief further fall (sudden drop in intrathoracic pressure)</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>IV (recovery)</td><td><strong>Overshoot</strong> as restored cardiac output meets constricted vessels</td><td>Reflex <strong>bradycardia</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>A <strong>square-wave response</strong> (BP rises and stays up with no overshoot or heart rate change) occurs in heart failure; loss of the phase IV overshoot and bradycardia indicates <strong>autonomic neuropathy</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"response-to-haemorrhage\">Response to haemorrhage</h2>\n<ul><li>Baroreflex tachycardia and vasoconstriction (skin, gut, kidney) preserve flow to the brain and heart; venoconstriction mobilises blood from venous reservoirs.</li><li><strong>Transcapillary refill:</strong> lower capillary hydrostatic pressure draws interstitial fluid into the vessels (can restore about 0.5–1 L over hours).</li><li>Hormonal: catecholamines, ADH, RAAS, cortisol. Erythropoiesis restores red cells over weeks.</li><li>Blood pressure is often maintained until about 30% of blood volume is lost in healthy young patients, so a normal BP does not exclude major haemorrhage. Children and fit young adults may decompensate abruptly; β-blockers mask tachycardia.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> baroreceptor nerves; phases of the Valsalva; which hormones act on which receptors.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> a bleeding trauma patient with a \"normal\" blood pressure: explain the compensation and why you are worried.</li></ul></div>","cvs-cardiac-ap":"<h2 id=\"ventricular-myocyte-action-potential\">Ventricular myocyte action potential</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Phase</th><th>Event</th><th>Main current</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>4</td><td>Resting potential <strong>about −90 mV</strong></td><td>K<sup>+</sup> efflux through inward rectifier (I<sub>K1</sub>) channels</td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>Rapid depolarisation</td><td>Fast <strong>Na<sup>+</sup></strong> influx (voltage-gated Na<sup>+</sup> channels)</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Early partial repolarisation</td><td>Transient K<sup>+</sup> outflow (I<sub>to</sub>)</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td><strong>Plateau</strong> (~200 ms)</td><td><strong>Ca<sup>2+</sup> influx through L-type channels</strong> balanced by K<sup>+</sup> efflux</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Repolarisation</td><td>Delayed rectifier K<sup>+</sup> currents (I<sub>Kr</sub>, I<sub>Ks</sub>)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li>The long plateau gives a long <strong>absolute refractory period</strong>, so cardiac muscle cannot be tetanised and has time to relax and refill.</li><li><strong>Hyperkalaemia</strong> makes the resting potential less negative, inactivating some Na<sup>+</sup> channels, slowing conduction (wide QRS) and eventually causing ventricular fibrillation or asystole.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"pacemaker-sa-node-action-potential\">Pacemaker (SA node) action potential</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>No stable resting potential.</strong> Phase 4 is a slow diastolic depolarisation from about −60 mV, due mainly to the <strong>\"funny\" current (I<sub>f</sub>, HCN channels; Na<sup>+</sup> and K<sup>+</sup>)</strong> plus T-type Ca<sup>2+</sup> current.</li><li><strong>Phase 0</strong> in nodal tissue is caused by <strong>L-type Ca<sup>2+</sup></strong> influx (slower upstroke), not Na<sup>+</sup>.</li><li>Sympathetic stimulation (β<sub>1</sub>, cAMP) steepens phase 4 → faster rate. Vagal stimulation (M<sub>2</sub>, ACh → more K<sup>+</sup> conductance) hyperpolarises and flattens phase 4 → slower rate.</li><li><strong>Hierarchy of pacemakers:</strong> SA node 60–100/min, AV node 40–60/min, ventricular (Purkinje) 20–40/min.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"conduction\">Conduction</h2>\n<p>SA node → atria → <strong>AV node</strong> (slow conduction, about 0.1 s delay, allowing ventricular filling) → bundle of His → bundle branches → Purkinje fibres (fastest conduction, about 4 m/s).</p>\n<h2 id=\"excitation-contraction-coupling\">Excitation–contraction coupling</h2>\n<ul><li>The action potential spreads down <strong>T-tubules</strong>; L-type channels (dihydropyridine receptors) let in a small amount of Ca<sup>2+</sup>, which triggers a much larger release from the <strong>sarcoplasmic reticulum via ryanodine receptors (RyR2)</strong>: <strong>calcium-induced calcium release</strong>.</li><li>Ca<sup>2+</sup> binds <strong>troponin C</strong>, moving tropomyosin to expose myosin-binding sites on actin; cross-bridges cycle using ATP.</li><li>Relaxation: Ca<sup>2+</sup> is pumped back into the SR by <strong>SERCA</strong> (regulated by phospholamban) and expelled by the <strong>Na<sup>+</sup>/Ca<sup>2+</sup> exchanger</strong>.</li><li><strong>Digoxin</strong> inhibits Na<sup>+</sup>/K<sup>+</sup>-ATPase, raising intracellular Na<sup>+</sup> and hence Ca<sup>2+</sup> (reduced exchange) → positive inotropy.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> ions for each phase; why nodal tissue has a slow upstroke; effect of potassium on the ECG.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> explaining how a drug (atropine, a β-blocker, amiodarone) changes heart rate or rhythm, from the ion channel up.</li></ul></div>","cvs-cardiac-cycle":"<h2 id=\"phases-left-ventricle\">Phases (left ventricle)</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Isovolumetric contraction:</strong> mitral valve closes (S1); pressure rises with no change in volume until it exceeds aortic pressure.</li><li><strong>Ejection:</strong> aortic valve opens; rapid then reduced ejection. Stroke volume ~70 mL from an end-diastolic volume of ~120 mL (<strong>ejection fraction ≈ 60%</strong>).</li><li><strong>Isovolumetric relaxation:</strong> aortic valve closes (S2, with the dicrotic notch on the arterial trace); pressure falls with no change in volume.</li><li><strong>Filling:</strong> mitral valve opens; rapid passive filling, diastasis, then <strong>atrial systole</strong> (about 20% of filling at rest; more important in the stiff or hypertrophied ventricle and lost in atrial fibrillation).</li></ol>\n<p><strong>The CVP waveform:</strong> a wave (atrial contraction), c wave (tricuspid bulging during isovolumetric contraction), x descent (atrial relaxation and descent of the valve ring), v wave (atrial filling against a closed tricuspid valve), y descent (tricuspid opening). Cannon a waves occur in complete heart block and junctional rhythms; large v waves in tricuspid regurgitation; absent a waves in AF.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-pressure-volume-loop\">The pressure–volume loop</h2>\n<ul><li>Plotted with <strong>LV volume on the x-axis and pressure on the y-axis</strong>; the loop runs anticlockwise.</li><li>Bottom-left corner = mitral valve opens; bottom-right = mitral closes (end-diastolic point); top-right = aortic opens; top-left = aortic closes (end-systolic point).</li><li><strong>Width = stroke volume.</strong> <strong>Area = stroke work.</strong></li><li>The <strong>end-systolic pressure–volume relationship (ESPVR)</strong> is the line joining end-systolic points under different loading: its slope (<strong>end-systolic elastance</strong>) is a relatively load-independent index of <strong>contractility</strong>.</li><li>The <strong>end-diastolic pressure–volume relationship (EDPVR)</strong> describes passive filling: a steeper curve means a stiffer ventricle (diastolic dysfunction).</li><li><strong>Arterial elastance</strong> (Ea ≈ end-systolic pressure / stroke volume) represents afterload.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Change</th><th>Effect on the loop</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>↑ Preload</td><td>Wider loop; end-diastolic point moves right along the EDPVR</td></tr><tr><td>↑ Afterload</td><td>Taller, narrower loop; smaller stroke volume</td></tr><tr><td>↑ Contractility</td><td>Steeper ESPVR; wider loop with smaller end-systolic volume</td></tr><tr><td>Aortic stenosis</td><td>Very high systolic pressures; steep EDPVR (hypertrophy)</td></tr><tr><td>Mitral regurgitation</td><td>No true isovolumetric phases; large end-diastolic volume</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> identify points on a loop; what the ESPVR slope means; ejection fraction and load-dependence.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> draw a PV loop and show the effect of a vasopressor, a fluid bolus and an inotrope; explain why ejection fraction is load-dependent.</li></ul></div>","cvs-cardiac-output":"<h2 id=\"determinants\">Determinants</h2>\n<p><strong>CO = HR × SV.</strong> Resting adult CO ≈ 5 L/min; cardiac index ≈ 2.5–4 L/min/m<sup>2</sup>.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Preload:</strong> ventricular wall stretch at end-diastole (approximated by end-diastolic volume; pressures such as CVP are poor surrogates).</li><li><strong>Afterload:</strong> the wall stress the ventricle must generate during ejection. <strong>Laplace:</strong> wall stress ∝ <strong>P × r / (2 × wall thickness)</strong>, so a dilated ventricle faces higher afterload at the same pressure, and hypertrophy reduces wall stress.</li><li><strong>Contractility:</strong> the intrinsic strength of contraction at a given preload and afterload; increased by sympathetic stimulation and inotropes, reduced by ischaemia, acidosis, hypoxia and many anaesthetic drugs.</li><li><strong>Heart rate:</strong> increasing rate raises output until diastolic filling time (and coronary perfusion time) becomes limiting; the <strong>Bowditch (staircase) effect</strong> gives a small inotropic effect with higher rates.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"frank-starling-mechanism\">Frank–Starling mechanism</h2>\n<ul><li>Within physiological limits, <strong>greater end-diastolic fibre length → greater force of contraction</strong> (optimal actin–myosin overlap and increased calcium sensitivity of troponin).</li><li>Starling curves: stroke volume against preload. Sympathetic stimulation and inotropes shift the curve up and left; heart failure shifts it down and right.</li><li>On the steep part, a fluid bolus raises stroke volume (<strong>fluid responsive</strong>); on the flat part it only raises filling pressures and causes oedema.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"venous-return-guyton\">Venous return (Guyton)</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Venous return = (mean systemic filling pressure − right atrial pressure) / resistance to venous return.</strong> Mean systemic filling pressure is about 7 mmHg; about 70% of blood volume is in the veins.</li><li>At steady state, cardiac output equals venous return. Venoconstriction (sympathetic tone, vasopressors) raises mean systemic filling pressure and venous return; anaesthetic vasodilation lowers it.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-fluid-challenge\">The fluid challenge</h2>\n<ul><li>Give a defined bolus (for example 250 mL crystalloid over 5–10 minutes) and measure a <strong>stroke volume</strong> response (a rise of 10% or more is commonly used) rather than relying on blood pressure or CVP.</li><li>Dynamic indices (pulse pressure or stroke volume variation during controlled ventilation, passive leg raising) predict response better than static pressures, within their limitations.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> Laplace and afterload; Starling curves; the Guyton venous return equation.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> \"This patient's blood pressure is 80/40 after induction. Talk me through the causes in terms of preload, afterload, contractility and rate, and what you would do.\"</li></ul></div>","cvs-fetal-circulation":"<h2 id=\"the-fetal-circulation\">The fetal circulation</h2>\n<ul><li>Oxygenated blood leaves the <strong>placenta</strong> in the <strong>umbilical vein</strong> (PO<sub>2</sub> only about 4 kPa, but saturation about 80% thanks to <strong>fetal haemoglobin</strong>, which has a <strong>P50 of about 2.5 kPa</strong> and does not bind 2,3-DPG well).</li><li><strong>Ductus venosus:</strong> about half of umbilical venous blood bypasses the liver to the IVC.</li><li><strong>Foramen ovale:</strong> the better-oxygenated IVC stream is directed across the atrial septum to the left atrium → left ventricle → <strong>brain and coronary arteries</strong>.</li><li><strong>Ductus arteriosus:</strong> most right ventricular output bypasses the high-resistance fluid-filled lungs from the pulmonary artery to the descending aorta.</li><li>Deoxygenated blood returns to the placenta in the <strong>two umbilical arteries</strong>.</li><li>Pulmonary vascular resistance is <strong>high</strong> and systemic (placental) resistance <strong>low</strong> before birth.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-placenta\">The placenta</h2>\n<ul><li>Gas exchange by <strong>diffusion</strong>, aided by the double Bohr effect (fetal CO<sub>2</sub> loss increases fetal affinity; maternal CO<sub>2</sub> gain decreases maternal affinity) and the double Haldane effect.</li><li>Transfer of drugs depends on lipid solubility, ionisation, protein binding and molecular size. Most anaesthetic drugs cross; <strong>neuromuscular blockers (quaternary, ionised)</strong> and heparin do not cross to any significant degree. <strong>Ion trapping:</strong> weak bases such as local anaesthetics become more ionised in the more acidic fetus, especially when it is acidotic.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"changes-at-birth\">Changes at birth</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>First breaths</strong> expand the lungs; alveolar PO<sub>2</sub> rises and PVR falls dramatically.</li><li><strong>Clamping the cord</strong> removes the low-resistance placenta, so SVR rises.</li><li>Left atrial pressure now exceeds right, closing the <strong>flap of the foramen ovale</strong> (functional closure at once; anatomical closure over months; probe-patent in about a quarter of adults).</li><li>The <strong>ductus arteriosus</strong> constricts within hours to days in response to higher PO<sub>2</sub> and falling prostaglandins (functional closure in 1–3 days). NSAIDs such as indometacin or ibuprofen promote closure; prostaglandin E infusions keep it open in duct-dependent congenital heart disease.</li><li>The <strong>ductus venosus</strong> closes over days.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Transitional circulation:</strong> hypoxia, acidosis, hypothermia or sepsis in the neonate can raise PVR again and reopen right-to-left shunting (persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn).</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> the three shunts and their fate; fetal haemoglobin P50; which drugs cross the placenta.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> explaining to a colleague why avoiding hypoxia, hypercapnia and hypothermia matters in a neonate having surgery.</li></ul></div>","cvs-microcirculation":"<h2 id=\"structure\">Structure</h2>\n<p>Arterioles (the main site of <strong>resistance</strong>, under neural, humoral and local control) → capillaries (exchange) → venules. Precapillary sphincters and arteriolar tone control how many capillaries are perfused; in exercising muscle, recruitment can increase flow many times over.</p>\n<h2 id=\"starling-s-equation\">Starling's equation</h2>\n<p><strong>Jv = Kf [(Pc − Pi) − σ(πc − πi)]</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Jv: net fluid filtration; Kf: filtration coefficient (surface area × permeability).</li><li>Pc and Pi: capillary and interstitial hydrostatic pressures.</li><li>πc and πi: plasma and interstitial oncotic pressures (plasma about 25 mmHg, mainly <strong>albumin</strong>).</li><li>σ: the reflection coefficient for protein (1 = impermeable, 0 = freely permeable).</li></ul>\n<p><strong>The revised Starling principle:</strong> the endothelial <strong>glycocalyx</strong> (a gel layer of glycoproteins and proteoglycans on the luminal surface) means the relevant oncotic gradient is between plasma and the small space just beneath the glycocalyx, not the whole interstitium. In most capillaries there is <strong>net filtration along the whole length, with little or no reabsorption</strong>; the filtrate returns via <strong>lymph</strong> (about 8 L/day enters lymphatics, most of which is returned to the circulation via lymph nodes and the thoracic duct). The glycocalyx is damaged by sepsis, surgery, hyperglycaemia and rapid volume loading.</p>\n<h2 id=\"causes-of-oedema\">Causes of oedema</h2>\n<ul><li>↑ capillary hydrostatic pressure: heart failure, venous obstruction, fluid overload.</li><li>↓ plasma oncotic pressure: hypoalbuminaemia (liver disease, nephrotic syndrome, malnutrition).</li><li>↑ permeability / glycocalyx damage: sepsis, burns, inflammation, ARDS.</li><li>Lymphatic obstruction.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"functions-of-the-endothelium\">Functions of the endothelium</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Vascular tone:</strong> releases <strong>nitric oxide</strong> (from L-arginine by eNOS, raising cGMP in smooth muscle) and <strong>prostacyclin</strong> (vasodilators), and <strong>endothelin-1</strong> (vasoconstrictor).</li><li><strong>Haemostasis:</strong> normally anticoagulant (thrombomodulin, heparan sulphate, tissue factor pathway inhibitor, tPA); becomes procoagulant when damaged (von Willebrand factor, tissue factor).</li><li>Barrier function, inflammation (adhesion molecules), angiogenesis, and metabolism (ACE in the lung).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> the Starling equation and its terms; the glycocalyx; nitric oxide synthesis.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> explaining why a septic patient develops oedema despite a normal albumin, and the implications for fluid therapy.</li></ul></div>","cvs-special-circulations":"<h2 id=\"share-of-cardiac-output-at-rest-approximate\">Share of cardiac output at rest (approximate)</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Organ</th><th>Flow</th><th>% of CO</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Liver and gut</td><td>~1.3–1.5 L/min</td><td>~25–30%</td><td>Dual supply</td></tr><tr><td>Kidneys</td><td>~1.1–1.2 L/min</td><td>~20–25%</td><td>Highest flow per gram</td></tr><tr><td>Brain</td><td>~750 mL/min</td><td>~15%</td><td>~50 mL/100 g/min</td></tr><tr><td>Heart</td><td>~250 mL/min</td><td>~5%</td><td>~70–80 mL/100 g/min</td></tr><tr><td>Muscle</td><td>~1 L/min</td><td>~20%</td><td>Up to 20-fold increase in exercise</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"coronary\">Coronary</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>LV perfusion occurs mainly in diastole</strong> because systolic intramyocardial pressure compresses subendocardial vessels. <strong>Coronary perfusion pressure ≈ aortic diastolic pressure − LVEDP.</strong> The RV is perfused through systole and diastole.</li><li>Myocardial <strong>oxygen extraction is already ~70% at rest</strong>, so extra demand must be met by <strong>increased flow</strong> (metabolic autoregulation: adenosine, NO, low PO<sub>2</sub>, K<sup>+</sup>, CO<sub>2</sub>).</li><li>Tachycardia shortens diastole (reduces supply) while increasing demand; the subendocardium is most vulnerable.</li><li>Anatomy: left main → LAD and circumflex; RCA supplies the SA node in about 60% and the AV node in about 80–90% (right dominance).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"cerebral\">Cerebral</h2>\n<ul><li>Autoregulation keeps flow roughly constant between a <strong>MAP of about 50–150 mmHg</strong> (shifted right in chronic hypertension; impaired after brain injury and by volatile agents at high concentration).</li><li><strong>CO<sub>2</sub> is the most powerful regulator:</strong> flow changes by about <strong>15 mL/100 g/min per kPa</strong> of PaCO<sub>2</sub> (roughly 2–4% per mmHg) between about 3 and 10 kPa. Hypoxia (PaO<sub>2</sub> below ~6.7 kPa) greatly increases flow.</li><li><strong>CPP = MAP − ICP</strong> (or − CVP if higher).</li><li>Flow–metabolism coupling: flow follows metabolic rate (reduced by propofol, barbiturates and hypothermia).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"renal\">Renal</h2>\n<ul><li>High flow serves filtration, not oxygen need: extraction is low overall, but the <strong>medulla</strong> is relatively hypoxic and vulnerable.</li><li>Autoregulation (MAP ~ 75–160 mmHg) by the <strong>myogenic response</strong> and <strong>tubuloglomerular feedback</strong>.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"hepatic\">Hepatic</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Portal vein ~75% of flow</strong> (but ~50% of oxygen) and <strong>hepatic artery ~25% of flow</strong> (~50% of oxygen).</li><li>The <strong>hepatic arterial buffer response</strong>: when portal flow falls, hepatic arterial flow rises (adenosine washout).</li><li>No true autoregulation of portal flow. Flow is reduced by IPPV, PEEP, hypocapnia, sympathetic stimulation and neuraxial blockade with hypotension.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> percentages of cardiac output; coronary perfusion pressure; CO<sub>2</sub> reactivity; hepatic dual supply.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> a patient with ischaemic heart disease becomes tachycardic under anaesthesia: explain why this is dangerous and what you would do.</li></ul></div>","endocrine-glands":"<h2 id=\"hormone-signalling\">Hormone signalling</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Peptide and catecholamine hormones</strong> act on cell surface receptors (G-protein coupled or tyrosine kinase). <strong>Steroid and thyroid hormones</strong> cross the membrane and act on <strong>intracellular (nuclear) receptors</strong>, changing gene transcription (slower onset, longer effect).</li><li>Most axes use <strong>negative feedback</strong>; oxytocin in labour is an example of positive feedback.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"pituitary\">Pituitary</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Anterior</strong> (adenohypophysis; hypothalamic releasing hormones via the portal circulation): ACTH, TSH, GH, prolactin, FSH, LH.</li><li><strong>Posterior</strong> (neurohypophysis; axons from the hypothalamus): <strong>ADH</strong> and <strong>oxytocin</strong>.</li><li>Anatomy: in the sella turcica, below the optic chiasm (bitemporal hemianopia), with the cavernous sinuses laterally. Acromegaly (excess GH) causes airway difficulty (macroglossia, prognathism, laryngeal changes) and OSA.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"adrenal-gland\">Adrenal gland</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Zone</th><th>Hormone</th><th>Controlled by</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Zona glomerulosa</td><td><strong>Aldosterone</strong></td><td>Angiotensin II, K<sup>+</sup></td></tr><tr><td>Zona fasciculata</td><td><strong>Cortisol</strong></td><td>ACTH</td></tr><tr><td>Zona reticularis</td><td>Androgens</td><td>ACTH</td></tr><tr><td>Medulla</td><td><strong>Adrenaline</strong>, noradrenaline</td><td>Preganglionic sympathetic fibres (ACh)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li><strong>Cortisol</strong> (about 20 mg/day, far more under stress): gluconeogenesis, anti-inflammatory, permissive for catecholamine vasoconstriction. <strong>Adrenal insufficiency</strong> (Addison's, or suppression by exogenous steroids) → hypotension resistant to vasopressors, hyponatraemia, hyperkalaemia, hypoglycaemia; give <strong>hydrocortisone</strong>. Patients on long-term steroids need perioperative steroid cover.</li><li><strong>Phaeochromocytoma:</strong> catecholamine-secreting tumour; requires preoperative <strong>α-blockade first</strong>, then β-blockade.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"thyroid\">Thyroid</h2>\n<ul><li>Iodide trapping → thyroglobulin iodination (thyroid peroxidase) → <strong>T<sub>4</sub></strong> (mostly) and <strong>T<sub>3</sub></strong> (more active; formed peripherally by deiodination). Highly protein-bound.</li><li>Increase metabolic rate, oxygen consumption, heat production, heart rate (upregulated β receptors).</li><li><strong>Hyperthyroidism:</strong> risk of <strong>thyroid storm</strong> perioperatively (fever, tachycardia, AF, agitation) — make euthyroid before elective surgery. <strong>Hypothyroidism:</strong> sensitivity to anaesthetic drugs, hypothermia, hypoventilation.</li><li>Anatomy: the <strong>recurrent laryngeal nerves</strong> run close to the inferior thyroid arteries (at risk in thyroidectomy); the <strong>parathyroids</strong> lie behind.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"calcium-homeostasis\">Calcium homeostasis</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>PTH</strong> (low Ca<sup>2+</sup> stimulates): increases bone resorption, renal Ca<sup>2+</sup> reabsorption and phosphate excretion, and renal <strong>1α-hydroxylation</strong> of vitamin D.</li><li><strong>Vitamin D (calcitriol):</strong> increases gut absorption of Ca<sup>2+</sup> and phosphate.</li><li><strong>Calcitonin</strong> (thyroid C cells): lowers Ca<sup>2+</sup> (minor role in humans).</li><li>Post-thyroidectomy <strong>hypocalcaemia</strong> (parathyroid damage): perioral tingling, Chvostek's and Trousseau's signs, laryngospasm, prolonged QT.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> adrenal zones; T<sub>3</sub> versus T<sub>4</sub>; PTH actions; nuclear versus membrane receptors.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> planning anaesthesia for a patient on long-term prednisolone, or recognising stridor after thyroidectomy.</li></ul></div>","endocrine-stress-glucose":"<h2 id=\"insulin-and-glucagon\">Insulin and glucagon</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Insulin</strong> (pancreatic β cells; released by glucose via GLUT2 uptake → ATP → closure of K<sub>ATP</sub> channels → depolarisation → Ca<sup>2+</sup> entry): the only hormone that lowers glucose. Promotes glucose uptake (GLUT4 in muscle and fat), glycogen, fat and protein synthesis; shifts K<sup>+</sup> into cells; inhibits lipolysis and ketogenesis.</li><li><strong>Counter-regulatory hormones</strong> raise glucose: <strong>glucagon</strong> (α cells: glycogenolysis, gluconeogenesis, ketogenesis), <strong>adrenaline</strong>, <strong>cortisol</strong>, <strong>growth hormone</strong>.</li><li>Normal fasting plasma glucose about 4–6 mmol/L. The brain needs about <strong>120 g of glucose a day</strong> (it can adapt to ketones in prolonged starvation). Red cells depend entirely on glucose.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-stress-response-to-surgery\">The stress response to surgery</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>System</th><th>Change</th><th>Effects</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Hypothalamic–pituitary</td><td>↑ ACTH, GH, ADH, prolactin</td><td>↑ cortisol, water retention</td></tr><tr><td>Sympathetic / adrenal medulla</td><td>↑ catecholamines</td><td>Tachycardia, hypertension, hyperglycaemia</td></tr><tr><td>Pancreas</td><td>↑ glucagon, relative ↓ insulin, insulin resistance</td><td><strong>Hyperglycaemia</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Renin–angiotensin–aldosterone</td><td>↑</td><td>Na<sup>+</sup> and water retention, K<sup>+</sup> loss</td></tr><tr><td>Metabolic</td><td>Catabolism: protein breakdown, lipolysis, negative nitrogen balance</td><td>Muscle loss, delayed recovery</td></tr><tr><td>Inflammatory</td><td>IL-6 (peaks at about 24 h), acute phase proteins (CRP), fever</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li>The response is proportional to the size of surgery. It is reduced by <strong>neuraxial blockade</strong> (especially for lower body surgery, blocking afferent input), high-dose opioids, minimally invasive surgery and avoiding hypothermia; <strong>etomidate</strong> blocks cortisol synthesis. Enhanced recovery aims to limit it.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"starvation\">Starvation</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Early (first 24 h):</strong> hepatic glycogen (about 100 g) is exhausted within about a day.</li><li><strong>Days:</strong> gluconeogenesis from amino acids (muscle protein), lactate and glycerol; lipolysis releases fatty acids; ketogenesis increases.</li><li><strong>Prolonged:</strong> the brain adapts to use <strong>ketones</strong>, sparing protein; metabolic rate falls.</li><li><strong>Refeeding syndrome:</strong> in malnourished patients, feeding drives insulin release → intracellular shift of <strong>phosphate</strong>, potassium and magnesium, and thiamine depletion → arrhythmias, respiratory failure, Wernicke's encephalopathy. Start feeding slowly with electrolyte monitoring and thiamine.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"preoperative-fasting-and-carbohydrate-loading\">Preoperative fasting and carbohydrate loading</h2>\n<ul><li>Standard adult elective fasting: <strong>6 hours for food</strong>, <strong>2 hours for clear fluids</strong> (many units now allow clear fluids until called to theatre, following newer guidance); breast milk 4 h in infants; for children, clear fluids up to 1 hour before is endorsed by consensus.</li><li>Carbohydrate drinks before surgery reduce insulin resistance and thirst (with caution in diabetes).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> hormones of the stress response; mechanism of insulin release; stages of starvation; refeeding.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> managing a diabetic patient's glucose and medication around a morning operation.</li></ul></div>","fluids-osmolality":"<h2 id=\"compartments-70-kg-man\">Compartments (70 kg man)</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Total body water ≈ 60% of body weight (~42 L)</strong>: less in women and older people (more fat), about 75–80% in term neonates.</li><li><strong>Intracellular ≈ 2/3 (~28 L)</strong>; <strong>extracellular ≈ 1/3 (~14 L)</strong>: interstitial ~10.5 L, plasma ~3 L, plus transcellular fluid.</li><li><strong>Blood volume ≈ 70 mL/kg</strong> in adults (about 80–90 mL/kg in infants).</li><li>Measured by dilution: total body water (deuterium oxide), ECF (inulin, bromide), plasma (Evans blue, labelled albumin).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"osmolality-and-tonicity\">Osmolality and tonicity</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Osmolarity:</strong> osmoles per litre of solution. <strong>Osmolality:</strong> osmoles per kg of solvent (measured by freezing point depression). Normal plasma osmolality <strong>275–295 mOsm/kg</strong>.</li><li><strong>Calculated osmolality ≈ 2Na<sup>+</sup> + glucose + urea.</strong> An <strong>osmolar gap</strong> above about 10 suggests an unmeasured osmole (ethanol, methanol, ethylene glycol, mannitol).</li><li><strong>Tonicity</strong> is the effective osmolality: only solutes that cannot cross the membrane move water. Urea is an ineffective osmole; glucose is effective when insulin is lacking. <strong>5% dextrose is iso-osmolar in the bag but hypotonic in the body</strong> once the glucose is metabolised.</li><li>The <strong>colligative properties</strong> (osmotic pressure, freezing point depression, boiling point elevation, vapour pressure depression) depend on the number of particles, not their nature.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"electrolytes\">Electrolytes</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Ion</th><th>Normal plasma</th><th>Key roles and points</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Na<sup>+</sup></td><td>135–145 mmol/L</td><td>Main ECF cation; determines ECF volume. Correct chronic hyponatraemia slowly (limit about 8–10 mmol/L per 24 h) to avoid osmotic demyelination</td></tr><tr><td>K<sup>+</sup></td><td>3.5–5.0 mmol/L</td><td>Main ICF cation (~150 mmol/L inside). Shifted into cells by insulin, β<sub>2</sub> agonists and alkalosis. Both high and low levels cause arrhythmias</td></tr><tr><td>Ca<sup>2+</sup></td><td>Total 2.2–2.6 mmol/L; ionised ~1.1–1.3</td><td>About 45% bound to albumin; alkalosis increases binding and lowers ionised Ca<sup>2+</sup>. Citrate in transfused blood binds Ca<sup>2+</sup></td></tr><tr><td>Mg<sup>2+</sup></td><td>0.7–1.0 mmol/L</td><td>Cofactor for ATP; NMDA and calcium channel antagonism; used in pre-eclampsia, asthma, torsades and arrhythmias. Potentiates neuromuscular block</td></tr><tr><td>Cl<sup>−</sup></td><td>95–105 mmol/L</td><td>Affects acid–base (Stewart)</td></tr><tr><td>PO<sub>4</sub><sup>3−</sup></td><td>0.8–1.5 mmol/L</td><td>ATP, 2,3-DPG; falls in refeeding syndrome</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"unit-conversions\">Unit conversions</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>1% = 10 mg/mL</strong> (1 g per 100 mL). So 2% lidocaine = 20 mg/mL; 1:1000 adrenaline = 1 mg/mL; 1:200,000 = 5 µg/mL.</li><li>mmol = mass (mg) ÷ molecular weight. 1 g of NaCl ≈ 17 mmol Na<sup>+</sup>; 0.9% saline contains 154 mmol/L each of Na<sup>+</sup> and Cl<sup>−</sup>.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> compartment volumes; osmolality versus tonicity; calculation of osmolar gap; % and ratio conversions.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> calculating a safe dose of a drug expressed as a percentage, or explaining why 5% dextrose is a poor resuscitation fluid.</li></ul></div>","immunity-anaphylaxis":"<h2 id=\"immunity\">Immunity</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Innate:</strong> barriers, neutrophils, macrophages, NK cells, <strong>complement</strong> (classical, alternative and lectin pathways), mast cells and acute phase proteins. Rapid, non-specific, no memory.</li><li><strong>Adaptive:</strong> <strong>B cells</strong> (antibodies: humoral) and <strong>T cells</strong> (CD4 helper, CD8 cytotoxic: cellular). Specific, with memory.</li><li><strong>Antibodies:</strong> IgG (most abundant; crosses the placenta), IgM (first response, pentamer, fixes complement), IgA (mucosal), <strong>IgE</strong> (mast cells and basophils; allergy and parasites), IgD.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"hypersensitivity-gell-and-coombs\">Hypersensitivity (Gell and Coombs)</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Mechanism</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>I (immediate)</td><td><strong>IgE</strong> on mast cells → degranulation</td><td>Anaphylaxis, asthma, hay fever</td></tr><tr><td>II (cytotoxic)</td><td>IgG/IgM against cell surface antigens</td><td>ABO transfusion reaction, haemolytic disease of the newborn, heparin-induced thrombocytopenia</td></tr><tr><td>III (immune complex)</td><td>Antigen–antibody complexes deposit</td><td>Serum sickness, lupus</td></tr><tr><td>IV (delayed)</td><td>T cell-mediated</td><td>Contact dermatitis, chlorhexidine skin reactions, tuberculin test</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"anaphylaxis\">Anaphylaxis</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Allergic (IgE-mediated) anaphylaxis</strong> requires prior sensitisation. <strong>Non-allergic anaphylaxis</strong> (previously \"anaphylactoid\") results from direct mast cell activation (e.g. via the MRGPRX2 receptor) or complement, and can occur on first exposure. Clinically they are managed identically.</li><li>Mediators: <strong>histamine</strong>, tryptase, leukotrienes, prostaglandins, PAF → vasodilation, capillary leak (up to half the plasma volume within minutes), bronchospasm, angioedema.</li><li>Perioperative presentation: <strong>hypotension</strong> is the commonest first feature; bronchospasm, rising airway pressure, falling ETCO<sub>2</sub>, rash (may be absent), and cardiac arrest. Most reactions occur within minutes of induction.</li><li><strong>NAP6</strong> (UK, 2018): incidence about <strong>1 in 10,000 anaesthetics</strong>; commonest triggers <strong>antibiotics</strong> (especially teicoplanin and co-amoxiclav), <strong>neuromuscular blockers</strong> (notably suxamethonium and rocuronium; atracurium less often) and <strong>chlorhexidine</strong>, then patent blue dye. Most deaths were in older patients with cardiovascular disease, often on β-blockers or ACE inhibitors.</li><li><strong>Treatment:</strong> adrenaline is first-line (IV titrated boluses under anaesthesia, e.g. 50 µg in adults, or IM 0.5 mg if no IV access), fluids, and remove triggers — follow the QRH and RCUK guidance. See the clinical note on managing anaphylaxis.</li><li><strong>Investigation:</strong> mast cell <strong>tryptase</strong> samples at about <strong>1 hour</strong> (up to 2 h) and <strong>at least 24 hours</strong> after the reaction (baseline), with an initial sample as soon as resuscitation allows; refer to an allergy clinic; tell the patient and GP and report to MHRA.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> antibody classes; hypersensitivity types; tryptase timing; NAP6 triggers.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> managing and then explaining an anaphylactic reaction to the patient afterwards, including follow-up and what they must tell future anaesthetists.</li></ul></div>","liver-gi":"<h2 id=\"the-liver\">The liver</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Carbohydrate:</strong> glycogen storage, gluconeogenesis. <strong>Fat:</strong> β-oxidation, ketogenesis, lipoprotein and cholesterol synthesis. <strong>Protein:</strong> albumin, clotting factors (except VIII, made mainly in endothelium), deamination and the <strong>urea cycle</strong> (ammonia → urea).</li><li><strong>Drug metabolism</strong> (phase I and II), bilirubin conjugation and excretion, bile production (fat absorption), hormone inactivation, storage of vitamins A, D, B<sub>12</sub> and iron, and Kupffer cell immune function.</li><li><strong>Liver function tests:</strong> albumin and INR reflect <strong>synthetic function</strong>; transaminases (ALT, AST) hepatocellular injury; ALP and GGT cholestasis; bilirubin excretion.</li><li>Anaesthetic relevance of liver disease: coagulopathy, hypoalbuminaemia (more free drug), encephalopathy (sensitivity to sedatives), ascites, portal hypertension and varices, hepatorenal and hepatopulmonary syndromes, altered drug handling.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"gastric-physiology\">Gastric physiology</h2>\n<ul><li>Parietal cells secrete <strong>HCl</strong> (proton pump; stimulated by <strong>gastrin</strong>, histamine via H<sub>2</sub> receptors and ACh via M<sub>3</sub>) and intrinsic factor; chief cells secrete pepsinogen. About 2 L of gastric juice a day.</li><li><strong>Gastric emptying:</strong> clear fluids leave with a half-time of about 10–20 minutes; solids take hours, fatty meals longest. Emptying is delayed by opioids, pain, anxiety, labour, diabetes (autonomic neuropathy), trauma, bowel obstruction and pregnancy near term.</li><li><strong>Lower oesophageal sphincter</strong> tone protects against reflux (barrier pressure = LOS pressure − gastric pressure). Reduced by pregnancy, opioids, anticholinergics, propofol and volatiles; increased by metoclopramide and suxamethonium (which also raises gastric pressure, so barrier pressure is maintained).</li><li><strong>Aspiration risk</strong> is increased by a full stomach, delayed emptying, reflux, obesity, pregnancy, obstruction and reduced consciousness. Strategies include fasting, antacids and H<sub>2</sub> blockers or PPIs, nasogastric drainage, RSI, and extubation awake.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"nausea-and-vomiting\">Nausea and vomiting</h2>\n<ul><li>Coordinated by the <strong>vomiting centre</strong> (the \"central pattern generator\" in the medulla, around the nucleus tractus solitarius), with inputs from:<ul><li>the <strong>chemoreceptor trigger zone</strong> in the <strong>area postrema</strong> (outside the blood–brain barrier; D<sub>2</sub>, 5-HT<sub>3</sub>, NK<sub>1</sub> and opioid receptors) → opioids, toxins;</li><li><strong>vagal afferents</strong> from the gut (5-HT<sub>3</sub>; serotonin release by enterochromaffin cells);</li><li>the <strong>vestibular system</strong> (H<sub>1</sub>, muscarinic) → motion;</li><li>the cortex (anticipation, smells, pain).</li></ul></li><li>Each antiemetic targets one or more of these receptors, which is why combinations work better.</li><li><strong>PONV risk factors (Apfel, adults):</strong> female sex, non-smoker, history of PONV or motion sickness, postoperative opioids. Also: volatile agents and nitrous oxide, longer surgery, certain operations (e.g. laparoscopic gynaecological, strabismus), and age (children over 3 and young adults).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> stimuli for acid secretion; factors affecting gastric emptying and LOS tone; receptors in the CTZ.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> assessing aspiration risk before an emergency laparotomy and explaining your plan; building a PONV prophylaxis plan.</li></ul></div>","neuro-ans":"<h2 id=\"anatomy\">Anatomy</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Sympathetic</th><th>Parasympathetic</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Outflow</td><td><strong>Thoracolumbar (T1–L2)</strong></td><td><strong>Craniosacral</strong>: cranial nerves III, VII, IX, X and S2–4</td></tr><tr><td>Ganglia</td><td>Paravertebral chain (and prevertebral: coeliac, mesenteric) — close to the cord</td><td>In or near the target organ</td></tr><tr><td>Preganglionic fibre</td><td>Short, myelinated (B fibres), via <strong>white rami</strong></td><td>Long</td></tr><tr><td>Postganglionic fibre</td><td>Long, unmyelinated (C fibres), via <strong>grey rami</strong> to spinal nerves</td><td>Short</td></tr><tr><td>Ganglionic transmitter</td><td>ACh (nicotinic)</td><td>ACh (nicotinic)</td></tr><tr><td>Postganglionic transmitter</td><td><strong>Noradrenaline</strong> (except sweat glands: ACh, muscarinic)</td><td><strong>ACh</strong> (muscarinic)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li>The <strong>adrenal medulla</strong> is a modified sympathetic ganglion: preganglionic fibres release ACh onto chromaffin cells, which secrete mainly <strong>adrenaline</strong> (about 80%) into the blood.</li><li>The <strong>stellate ganglion</strong> (fused inferior cervical and first thoracic ganglia) lies anterior to the neck of the first rib at C7; blocking it causes <strong>Horner's syndrome</strong> (ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis) and can treat upper limb sympathetic pain.</li><li>Cardiac sympathetic fibres come from <strong>T1–T4</strong>: a high neuraxial block above T4 removes cardiac accelerator fibres, allowing bradycardia.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"receptors\">Receptors</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Receptor</th><th>Second messenger</th><th>Main effects</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>α<sub>1</sub></td><td>G<sub>q</sub> → IP<sub>3</sub>/DAG → ↑ Ca<sup>2+</sup></td><td>Vasoconstriction, mydriasis, bladder sphincter contraction</td></tr><tr><td>α<sub>2</sub></td><td>G<sub>i</sub> → ↓ cAMP</td><td>Presynaptic inhibition of NA release; central sedation and analgesia (clonidine, dexmedetomidine)</td></tr><tr><td>β<sub>1</sub></td><td>G<sub>s</sub> → ↑ cAMP</td><td>↑ Heart rate, contractility, conduction; renin release</td></tr><tr><td>β<sub>2</sub></td><td>G<sub>s</sub> → ↑ cAMP</td><td>Bronchodilation, vasodilation (muscle), uterine relaxation, glycogenolysis, K<sup>+</sup> shift into cells</td></tr><tr><td>M<sub>2</sub></td><td>G<sub>i</sub></td><td>↓ Heart rate and AV conduction</td></tr><tr><td>M<sub>3</sub></td><td>G<sub>q</sub></td><td>Bronchoconstriction, secretions, gut motility, bladder contraction, endothelial NO (vasodilation)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"transmitter-handling\">Transmitter handling</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Noradrenaline:</strong> tyrosine → DOPA → dopamine → noradrenaline (→ adrenaline by PNMT in the adrenal medulla). Action ended mainly by <strong>reuptake (uptake 1)</strong> into the nerve terminal, then metabolism by <strong>MAO</strong> and <strong>COMT</strong> (urinary VMA).</li><li><strong>ACh:</strong> from choline and acetyl-CoA (choline acetyltransferase); hydrolysed by acetylcholinesterase.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"physiological-balance\">Physiological balance</h2>\n<p>Most organs have dual innervation; at rest the heart is under dominant <strong>vagal tone</strong> (intrinsic SA rate about 100/min falls to about 70/min), while vessels are under <strong>sympathetic tone</strong> (few blood vessels have parasympathetic innervation). Loss of sympathetic tone with neuraxial block or anaesthesia → vasodilation and hypotension.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> receptor subtypes and second messengers; transmitter synthesis and metabolism; anatomy of the chain and stellate ganglion.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> explaining bradycardia and hypotension after a high spinal for caesarean section, and choosing between phenylephrine, ephedrine and atropine.</li></ul></div>","neuro-icp-cbf":"<h2 id=\"monro-kellie-doctrine\">Monro–Kellie doctrine</h2>\n<p>The skull is a rigid box containing <strong>brain (~80%), blood (~10%) and CSF (~10%)</strong>. An increase in one component (tumour, haematoma, oedema) must be offset by a decrease in another, first by displacing CSF into the spinal subarachnoid space and venous blood out of the skull. Once compensation is exhausted, <strong>ICP rises steeply</strong> for small increases in volume (the exponential <strong>ICP–volume (elastance) curve</strong>).</p>\n<ul><li>Normal ICP: <strong>5–15 mmHg</strong> (supine adult).</li><li><strong>CPP = MAP − ICP</strong> (or − CVP if higher). Adult targets after severe head injury are usually a CPP of about <strong>60–70 mmHg</strong> and ICP under about <strong>20–22 mmHg</strong>.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"regulation-of-cerebral-blood-flow\">Regulation of cerebral blood flow</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Autoregulation</strong> holds flow roughly constant over a range of MAP (classically 50–150 mmHg; wider variation between individuals than once thought), via myogenic and metabolic mechanisms.</li><li><strong>PaCO<sub>2</sub></strong>: the most potent short-term controller (vasodilation with hypercapnia). Hyperventilation lowers ICP within minutes but risks ischaemia; it is reserved for short-term rescue of impending herniation.</li><li><strong>PaO<sub>2</sub></strong>: flow rises sharply below about 6.7–8 kPa.</li><li><strong>Metabolic coupling:</strong> CMRO<sub>2</sub> about 3.5 mL/100 g/min; reduced by propofol, barbiturates, hypothermia; increased by seizures and fever.</li><li><strong>Volatile agents</strong> are cerebral vasodilators (dose-dependent, increasing flow while reducing metabolism: \"uncoupling\"); propofol preserves coupling and reduces flow and ICP. Ketamine was traditionally avoided but has little effect on ICP when ventilation is controlled.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"csf\">CSF</h2>\n<ul><li>About <strong>500 mL/day</strong> is produced, mainly by the <strong>choroid plexus</strong> (active secretion of Na<sup>+</sup>, Cl<sup>−</sup> and HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup>, with water following); total volume about <strong>150 mL</strong> (roughly half in the spinal compartment).</li><li>Circulation: lateral ventricles → foramina of Monro → third ventricle → cerebral aqueduct → fourth ventricle → foramina of Magendie and Luschka → subarachnoid space → reabsorbed through <strong>arachnoid granulations</strong> into the venous sinuses.</li><li>Compared with plasma: similar Na<sup>+</sup>, <strong>higher Cl<sup>−</sup></strong>, <strong>lower glucose (~60% of plasma)</strong>, lower K<sup>+</sup>, very little protein, slightly lower pH. CSF pH is the stimulus for central chemoreceptors.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"signs-of-raised-icp-and-herniation\">Signs of raised ICP and herniation</h2>\n<p>Headache, vomiting, papilloedema, falling GCS; <strong>Cushing's response</strong> (hypertension, bradycardia, irregular breathing) is a late sign of brainstem compression; a fixed dilated pupil suggests uncal herniation compressing the oculomotor nerve.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> the ICP–volume curve; CPP calculation; effect of anaesthetic drugs on CBF and CMRO<sub>2</sub>; CSF composition.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> a head-injured patient before transfer: what physiological targets (PaO<sub>2</sub>, PaCO<sub>2</sub>, MAP, glucose, temperature, head position) protect the brain, and why?</li></ul></div>","neuro-membrane-ap":"<h2 id=\"resting-membrane-potential\">Resting membrane potential</h2>\n<ul><li>Set mainly by <strong>K<sup>+</sup> permeability</strong>: the resting membrane is far more permeable to K<sup>+</sup> than to Na<sup>+</sup>, so the potential (about <strong>−70 mV in neurones, −90 mV in muscle</strong>) lies close to the K<sup>+</sup> equilibrium potential.</li><li><strong>Nernst equation</strong> (one ion): <strong>E = (RT / zF) ln([ion]<sub>out</sub> / [ion]<sub>in</sub>)</strong> ≈ 61.5 log<sub>10</sub>([out]/[in]) mV at 37°C for a monovalent cation. E<sub>K</sub> ≈ −90 mV; E<sub>Na</sub> ≈ +60 mV.</li><li><strong>Goldman–Hodgkin–Katz equation</strong> combines all permeant ions weighted by their permeability.</li><li>The <strong>Na<sup>+</sup>/K<sup>+</sup>-ATPase</strong> (3 Na<sup>+</sup> out, 2 K<sup>+</sup> in) maintains the gradients and is slightly electrogenic; it is not what generates most of the resting potential.</li><li><strong>Raising extracellular K<sup>+</sup></strong> makes the resting potential less negative (depolarises), because E<sub>K</sub> changes. Changing extracellular Na<sup>+</sup> has little effect at rest because the membrane is barely permeable to Na<sup>+</sup>.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-action-potential\">The action potential</h2>\n<ol><li>A stimulus depolarises the membrane to <strong>threshold (~−55 mV)</strong>.</li><li><strong>Voltage-gated Na<sup>+</sup> channels</strong> open (positive feedback): rapid depolarisation towards E<sub>Na</sub> (overshoot to about +30 mV).</li><li>Na<sup>+</sup> channels <strong>inactivate</strong> (the inactivation gate closes) within about 1 ms; <strong>voltage-gated K<sup>+</sup> channels</strong> open more slowly → repolarisation and a brief <strong>after-hyperpolarisation</strong>.</li><li><strong>Absolute refractory period:</strong> Na<sup>+</sup> channels inactivated; no stimulus can trigger another action potential. <strong>Relative refractory period:</strong> a larger stimulus is needed.</li><li><strong>All-or-none:</strong> intensity is coded by frequency, not amplitude.</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"conduction\">Conduction</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Fibre</th><th>Myelin</th><th>Diameter (µm)</th><th>Velocity (m/s)</th><th>Function</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Aα</td><td>Yes</td><td>12–20</td><td>70–120</td><td>Motor, proprioception</td></tr><tr><td>Aβ</td><td>Yes</td><td>5–12</td><td>30–70</td><td>Touch, pressure</td></tr><tr><td>Aγ</td><td>Yes</td><td>3–6</td><td>15–30</td><td>Muscle spindle motor</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Aδ</strong></td><td>Yes</td><td>2–5</td><td>12–30</td><td><strong>Fast, sharp pain</strong>, cold</td></tr><tr><td><strong>B</strong></td><td>Yes (thin)</td><td>&lt; 3</td><td>3–15</td><td><strong>Preganglionic autonomic</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>C</strong></td><td><strong>No</strong></td><td>0.4–1.2</td><td>0.5–2</td><td><strong>Slow, dull pain</strong>, warmth, postganglionic autonomic</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li>Velocity rises with <strong>diameter</strong> (lower internal resistance) and <strong>myelination</strong> (saltatory conduction between <strong>nodes of Ranvier</strong>, where Na<sup>+</sup> channels cluster).</li><li><strong>Local anaesthetic sensitivity:</strong> thin fibres are generally blocked before thick ones, and myelinated fibres before unmyelinated fibres of similar diameter (about three consecutive nodes must be blocked to stop conduction). Clinically the order is sympathetic (B) → temperature and pinprick (Aδ, C) → touch (Aβ) → motor (Aα), which is why sympathetic and sensory blocks extend higher than motor block after a spinal.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> Nernst calculations; effect of changing extracellular K<sup>+</sup> or Na<sup>+</sup>; fibre types; differential block.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> explaining why a spinal block tested with cold spray is higher than with light touch, and what that means for surgery.</li></ul></div>","neuro-nmj-muscle":"<h2 id=\"the-neuromuscular-junction\">The neuromuscular junction</h2>\n<ol><li>The motor nerve action potential opens <strong>voltage-gated Ca<sup>2+</sup> channels</strong> in the terminal.</li><li>Ca<sup>2+</sup> entry triggers <strong>exocytosis of acetylcholine</strong> (quanta of about 5,000–10,000 molecules per vesicle).</li><li>ACh crosses the cleft and binds the <strong>nicotinic receptor</strong> (a ligand-gated ion channel of five subunits: <strong>2α, β, δ, ε</strong> in adults; γ replaces ε in fetal and extrajunctional receptors). <strong>Both α subunits</strong> must be occupied to open the channel.</li><li>Na<sup>+</sup> in and K<sup>+</sup> out produce the <strong>end-plate potential</strong>; if it reaches threshold, a muscle action potential spreads.</li><li><strong>Acetylcholinesterase</strong> in the cleft hydrolyses ACh within about 1 ms.</li><li><strong>Large safety margin:</strong> about 70–80% of receptors must be blocked before twitch height falls, and over 90% for complete block.</li><li><strong>Prejunctional nicotinic receptors</strong> (α3β2) mobilise ACh during high-frequency stimulation; blocking them by non-depolarising drugs is thought to cause <strong>fade</strong>.</li><li><strong>Upregulation</strong> of extrajunctional (immature, fetal-type and α7) receptors after denervation, burns, prolonged immobility or upper motor neurone injury → exaggerated K<sup>+</sup> release with suxamethonium and resistance to non-depolarising drugs.</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"skeletal-muscle-excitation-contraction-coupling\">Skeletal muscle excitation–contraction coupling</h2>\n<ul><li>The action potential travels down the <strong>T-tubules</strong>; the <strong>dihydropyridine receptor</strong> (voltage sensor) is <strong>mechanically coupled</strong> to the <strong>ryanodine receptor (RyR1)</strong> on the sarcoplasmic reticulum, releasing Ca<sup>2+</sup> (no calcium-induced release needed, unlike the heart).</li><li>Ca<sup>2+</sup> binds <strong>troponin C</strong> → tropomyosin moves → cross-bridge cycling. ATP is needed both to detach myosin and to pump Ca<sup>2+</sup> back (SERCA).</li><li><strong>Malignant hyperthermia:</strong> abnormal RyR1 (or the dihydropyridine receptor, CACNA1S) → uncontrolled Ca<sup>2+</sup> release with volatile agents or suxamethonium.</li><li><strong>Motor unit:</strong> one motor neurone and all the fibres it supplies (few fibres for fine control, e.g. extraocular muscles; thousands in large limb muscles). Force is graded by recruitment and firing rate (summation, tetanus).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"smooth-muscle\">Smooth muscle</h2>\n<ul><li>No troponin; Ca<sup>2+</sup> binds <strong>calmodulin</strong>, which activates <strong>myosin light-chain kinase</strong>. Relaxation by myosin light-chain phosphatase.</li><li>Ca<sup>2+</sup> comes from the extracellular space and the SR (IP<sub>3</sub> receptors). Slow, sustained contraction (\"latch\" state) with low energy cost.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"disorders-relevant-to-anaesthesia\">Disorders relevant to anaesthesia</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Condition</th><th>Problem</th><th>Anaesthetic implications</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Myasthenia gravis</td><td>Antibodies to postjunctional ACh receptors</td><td>Sensitive to non-depolarising NMBs; resistant to suxamethonium; postoperative respiratory weakness</td></tr><tr><td>Lambert–Eaton syndrome</td><td>Antibodies to presynaptic Ca<sup>2+</sup> channels (often small-cell lung cancer)</td><td>Sensitive to both depolarising and non-depolarising NMBs</td></tr><tr><td>Muscular dystrophies</td><td>Muscle membrane defects</td><td>Suxamethonium can cause hyperkalaemia and rhabdomyolysis; cardiomyopathy</td></tr><tr><td>Myotonic dystrophy</td><td>Abnormal chloride channels (myotonia)</td><td>Suxamethonium may cause sustained contraction; sensitive to respiratory depressants</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> receptor subunits; safety margin; how burns and denervation change responses; skeletal versus smooth versus cardiac coupling.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> planning neuromuscular block for a patient with myasthenia gravis, or explaining why suxamethonium is avoided two weeks after a major burn.</li></ul></div>","neuro-pain-physiology":"<h2 id=\"nociceptors-and-afferents\">Nociceptors and afferents</h2>\n<ul><li>Free nerve endings responding to mechanical, thermal and chemical stimuli. <strong>Aδ fibres</strong> (thinly myelinated): fast, sharp, well-localised \"first pain\". <strong>C fibres</strong> (unmyelinated): slow, dull, burning, poorly localised \"second pain\".</li><li>Inflammatory mediators (bradykinin, prostaglandins, H<sup>+</sup>, histamine, serotonin, nerve growth factor) sensitise nociceptors: <strong>peripheral sensitisation</strong> → primary hyperalgesia.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-dorsal-horn\">The dorsal horn</h2>\n<ul><li>Afferents enter via the dorsal root, travel a segment or two in <strong>Lissauer's tract</strong> and synapse in the <strong>dorsal horn laminae</strong>: Aδ in laminae I and V; C fibres in laminae I and <strong>II (substantia gelatinosa)</strong>.</li><li>Transmitters: <strong>glutamate</strong> (AMPA receptors for fast transmission; <strong>NMDA</strong> receptors recruited with sustained input), <strong>substance P</strong> and <strong>CGRP</strong>.</li><li><strong>Gate control theory (Melzack and Wall):</strong> activity in large Aβ touch fibres activates inhibitory interneurones in lamina II that reduce nociceptive transmission (the basis for rubbing an injury and TENS).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"ascending-pathways\">Ascending pathways</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Spinothalamic tract:</strong> second-order neurones <strong>cross within one or two segments</strong> in the anterior white commissure and ascend in the anterolateral cord to the <strong>thalamus</strong> (lateral: discriminative; medial: affective), then to the somatosensory and cingulate cortex and insula.</li><li>Spinoreticular and spinomesencephalic tracts contribute to arousal and emotional aspects.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"descending-modulation\">Descending modulation</h2>\n<p>From the <strong>periaqueductal grey</strong> and <strong>rostroventral medulla</strong> to the dorsal horn, using <strong>endogenous opioids</strong>, <strong>noradrenaline</strong> (α<sub>2</sub>) and <strong>serotonin</strong>. This is a target for opioids, and for tricyclics, SNRIs and α<sub>2</sub>-agonists.</p>\n<h2 id=\"central-sensitisation-and-chronic-pain\">Central sensitisation and chronic pain</h2>\n<ul><li>Repeated C-fibre input → <strong>wind-up</strong> (NMDA-dependent increase in dorsal horn response), expanded receptive fields, <strong>secondary hyperalgesia</strong> and <strong>allodynia</strong> (pain from normally non-painful stimuli).</li><li><strong>Neuropathic pain</strong> follows damage to the nervous system: ectopic discharges, sodium channel changes, loss of inhibition. Treated with gabapentinoids, tricyclics and SNRIs rather than simple analgesics.</li><li><strong>Visceral pain</strong> is poorly localised and <strong>referred</strong> to the dermatomes that share spinal segments (e.g. the heart to T1–T4 and the left arm).</li><li><strong>Labour pain:</strong> first stage via visceral afferents to <strong>T10–L1</strong>; second stage adds somatic pain from the perineum via the <strong>pudendal nerve (S2–4)</strong>.</li><li>Acute pain that is poorly controlled after surgery is a risk factor for <strong>chronic post-surgical pain</strong>.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> fibre types and laminae; where the spinothalamic tract crosses; sites of action of analgesic classes; labour pain pathways.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> explaining multimodal analgesia to a patient, or planning analgesia for a patient with established neuropathic pain having surgery.</li></ul></div>","nutrition-metabolism":"<h2 id=\"cellular-energy\">Cellular energy</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Glycolysis</strong> (cytoplasm): glucose → 2 pyruvate, net <strong>2 ATP</strong>, no oxygen needed.</li><li><strong>Aerobic:</strong> pyruvate → acetyl-CoA → <strong>Krebs cycle</strong> (mitochondrial matrix) → NADH and FADH<sub>2</sub> → <strong>oxidative phosphorylation</strong> (electron transport chain, inner mitochondrial membrane) → about <strong>30–32 ATP</strong> per glucose in total.</li><li><strong>Anaerobic:</strong> pyruvate → <strong>lactate</strong> (regenerating NAD<sup>+</sup> so glycolysis can continue): only 2 ATP per glucose.</li><li><strong>Lactate</strong> is cleared mainly by the liver (Cori cycle) and kidneys. Raised lactate: <strong>type A</strong> (tissue hypoxia: shock, hypoperfusion) and <strong>type B</strong> (no hypoxia: adrenaline via β<sub>2</sub> stimulation of glycolysis, liver failure, metformin, drugs, malignancy). In sepsis, raised lactate reflects both.</li><li>Fats (β-oxidation) yield the most energy per gram; ketone bodies supply the brain in starvation.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"energy-requirements\">Energy requirements</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Substrate</th><th>Energy (kcal/g)</th><th>Respiratory quotient (CO<sub>2</sub> produced / O<sub>2</sub> consumed)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Carbohydrate</td><td>~4</td><td><strong>1.0</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Protein</td><td>~4</td><td>~0.8</td></tr><tr><td>Fat</td><td>~9</td><td><strong>~0.7</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Mixed diet</td><td>—</td><td>~0.8</td></tr><tr><td>Overfeeding / lipogenesis</td><td>—</td><td><strong>&gt; 1.0</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li>Basal requirement roughly <strong>25 kcal/kg/day</strong> (more with stress, sepsis, burns); protein about 1–1.5 g/kg/day in critical illness. Water about 25–30 mL/kg/day.</li><li><strong>Measurement:</strong> indirect calorimetry (O<sub>2</sub> consumption and CO<sub>2</sub> production) or predictive equations (e.g. Harris–Benedict).</li><li><strong>Overfeeding</strong> with carbohydrate raises CO<sub>2</sub> production (RQ &gt; 1), which can hinder weaning from ventilation.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"perioperative-nutrition\">Perioperative nutrition</h2>\n<ul><li>Malnutrition (weight loss, low BMI, poor intake) predicts complications, poor wound healing and infection. Screen (e.g. MUST) and optimise before major elective surgery.</li><li><strong>Enteral feeding</strong> is preferred to parenteral when the gut works (maintains mucosal integrity). Early oral intake after surgery is a principle of enhanced recovery.</li><li><strong>Parenteral nutrition:</strong> central access, risk of line infection, hyperglycaemia, hepatic dysfunction. Watch for <strong>refeeding syndrome</strong> (see starvation).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> ATP yields; RQ values; causes of type A and B lactic acidosis.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> a raised lactate in a patient on an adrenaline infusion: interpreting it and deciding what to do.</li></ul></div>","paeds-physiology":"<h2 id=\"airway\">Airway</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Large head and occiput</strong> (neck flexion when supine: a shoulder roll helps in infants), <strong>large tongue</strong>, floppy U-shaped epiglottis, high <strong>anterior larynx</strong> (C3–4 in infants; C5–6 in adults).</li><li>Traditionally the <strong>cricoid</strong> was described as the narrowest point; imaging suggests the glottis may be narrower in the sagittal plane, but the cricoid ring is the narrowest <strong>complete, non-distensible</strong> ring, so an oversized tube causes subglottic oedema. Resistance ∝ 1/r<sup>4</sup>: 1 mm of oedema greatly narrows a small airway.</li><li>Short trachea (about 4–5 cm in neonates) → easy endobronchial intubation. <strong>Obligate nasal breathers</strong> for the first few months.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"respiratory\">Respiratory</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Oxygen consumption about 6–8 mL/kg/min</strong> (roughly double the adult value) with a small FRC → <strong>rapid desaturation</strong>.</li><li>Horizontal ribs, compliant chest wall and fatigue-prone diaphragm (fewer type I fibres): increased work of breathing. Minute ventilation is driven by <strong>rate</strong> (tidal volume ~7 mL/kg is similar to adults).</li><li>Closing capacity may exceed FRC in young infants.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"cardiovascular\">Cardiovascular</h2>\n<ul><li>Stroke volume is relatively fixed (stiff, less compliant ventricles): <strong>cardiac output is rate-dependent</strong>, and <strong>bradycardia is dangerous</strong> (often a sign of hypoxia).</li><li>Dominant parasympathetic tone: vagal reflexes (laryngoscopy, suxamethonium, oculocardiac reflex).</li><li>Blood volume: <strong>neonate ~90 mL/kg, infant ~80 mL/kg, child ~70–75 mL/kg</strong>.</li><li>Hypotension is a <strong>late</strong> sign of hypovolaemia.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"fluids-glucose-and-temperature\">Fluids, glucose and temperature</h2>\n<ul><li>Maintenance (the \"4-2-1\" rule): <strong>4 mL/kg/h for the first 10 kg, 2 mL/kg/h for the next 10 kg, 1 mL/kg/h thereafter</strong> (i.e. 100/50/20 mL/kg/day). Use <strong>isotonic</strong> fluids (NICE NG29); hypotonic fluids risk hyponatraemia because of ADH release.</li><li>Neonates and infants have limited glycogen: risk of <strong>hypoglycaemia</strong> with prolonged fasting.</li><li>High surface area to volume ratio, little subcutaneous fat, brown fat thermogenesis only → <strong>rapid heat loss</strong>.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"drugs\">Drugs</h2>\n<ul><li>Larger ECF and total body water → larger volume of distribution for water-soluble drugs (e.g. suxamethonium dose is higher per kg in infants).</li><li>Immature hepatic enzymes and renal function in neonates → reduced clearance of many drugs. MAC is highest in infants (about 1–6 months).</li><li><strong>Weight estimates (APLS, ages 1–10):</strong> (age + 4) × 2 kg is a common simple formula; use measured weight when possible.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"useful-formulae-starting-points-only\">Useful formulae (starting points only)</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Uncuffed tracheal tube internal diameter (mm):</strong> age/4 + 4; <strong>cuffed:</strong> age/4 + 3.5 (cuffed tubes are now widely used).</li><li><strong>Oral tube length (cm):</strong> age/2 + 12; nasal: age/2 + 15.</li><li><strong>Spinal cord</strong> ends at about <strong>L3 at birth</strong>, reaching the adult level (L1–2) by about 1 year; the dural sac ends at about S3–4 in neonates (S1–2 in adults).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> airway differences; blood volumes; maintenance fluids; drug handling in neonates.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> preparing equipment and drug doses for a 5-year-old having tonsillectomy, and explaining the plan to a parent.</li></ul></div>","pk-csht-tci":"<h2 id=\"key-facts\">Key facts</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Context-sensitive half-time (CSHT):</strong> the time for the <strong>plasma</strong> concentration to fall by <strong>50%</strong> after stopping an infusion that has maintained a <strong>steady plasma concentration</strong>, where the \"context\" is the <strong>duration of the infusion</strong>.</li><li>It is <strong>not</strong> the elimination half-life. After a short infusion, the fall in plasma concentration is driven mainly by <strong>redistribution</strong> to peripheral compartments. After a long infusion, those compartments are full and drug returns from them to plasma, so the fall slows.</li><li>CSHT depends on the <strong>ratio of elimination to redistribution</strong>, not on either alone.</li><li><strong>Remifentanil</strong>: CSHT is about <strong>3–4 minutes and almost independent of infusion duration</strong>, because non-specific plasma and tissue esterases give it a very high clearance relative to its small volume of distribution.</li><li><strong>Fentanyl</strong>: CSHT rises steeply with duration (large, lipid-soluble peripheral stores). <strong>Alfentanil</strong> plateaus at a moderate value. <strong>Propofol</strong> rises only modestly over several hours because of its high clearance.</li><li>CSHT does not predict waking directly: the decrement needed for recovery may be more or less than 50% (the <strong>decrement time</strong> is the general concept, e.g. 80% decrement time).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"compartment-models-and-tci\">Compartment models and TCI</h2>\n<ul><li>Three-compartment model: central volume V1 (plasma and highly perfused tissue), a fast peripheral compartment V2 (muscle) and a slow peripheral compartment V3 (fat), linked by rate constants k12, k21, k13, k31, with elimination k10 from V1.</li><li>A <strong>target-controlled infusion (TCI)</strong> pump uses a population PK model to calculate the bolus and the decreasing infusion rates needed to reach and hold a chosen <strong>plasma (Cp)</strong> or <strong>effect-site (Ce)</strong> target. Effect-site targeting adds an equilibration constant, <strong>ke0</strong>.</li><li>Propofol models: <strong>Marsh</strong> (weight only; plasma targeting) and <strong>Schnider</strong> (age, weight, height and lean body mass; usually effect-site targeting). <strong>Eleveld</strong> is a newer general-purpose model covering a wide range of ages and weights. Remifentanil: <strong>Minto</strong>.</li><li>Predicted concentrations are population estimates. Real concentrations vary considerably between patients, so titrate to clinical effect and depth-of-anaesthesia monitoring where appropriate.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"clinical-relevance\">Clinical relevance</h2>\n<ul><li>Choose drugs whose offset suits the case: remifentanil for long cases needing rapid, predictable emergence; avoid long fentanyl infusions if early extubation matters.</li><li>Plan <strong>transition analgesia</strong> before stopping remifentanil, because its analgesic effect ends within minutes.</li><li>TIVA safety: a dedicated cannula that is visible throughout, anti-reflux valves, correct model and patient data, and syringe labelling. Disconnection or extravasation risks awareness, as NAP5 highlighted.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> CSHT definitions; comparing offset of propofol, fentanyl, alfentanil and remifentanil; model inputs; why Ce and Cp differ.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> \"Your supervisor asks why you chose remifentanil TIVA for a 6-hour operation. Explain, and tell them what you will do about analgesia at the end.\" Expect to sketch CSHT curves.</li></ul></div>","pregnancy-physiology":"<h2 id=\"cardiovascular\">Cardiovascular</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Change</th><th>Size (by term)</th><th>Implication</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cardiac output</td><td>↑ <strong>~40–50%</strong> (rate and stroke volume); further ↑ in labour and immediately after delivery (autotransfusion)</td><td>Risk for women with cardiac disease, especially at delivery</td></tr><tr><td>SVR</td><td>↓ ~20% (progesterone, prostacyclin, low-resistance placenta)</td><td>BP falls in the second trimester</td></tr><tr><td>Blood volume</td><td>↑ <strong>~40–50%</strong> (plasma more than red cells)</td><td>Physiological (dilutional) anaemia</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Aortocaval compression</strong></td><td>From about 20 weeks when supine</td><td>Hypotension, reduced uteroplacental flow: use <strong>left lateral tilt</strong> or uterine displacement</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"respiratory\">Respiratory</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Minute ventilation ↑ ~45–50%</strong> (mainly tidal volume; progesterone increases CO<sub>2</sub> sensitivity) → <strong>PaCO<sub>2</sub> about 4.0–4.2 kPa</strong>, with renal compensation (bicarbonate ~18–21 mmol/L).</li><li><strong>FRC ↓ ~20%</strong> (more when supine) and <strong>O<sub>2</sub> consumption ↑ ~20–30%</strong> → rapid desaturation during apnoea.</li><li><strong>Airway:</strong> mucosal oedema and capillary engorgement (bleeding with nasal instrumentation; use smaller tracheal tubes), breast enlargement, weight gain. Failed intubation is more common than in the general population: follow the <strong>OAA/DAS obstetric guidelines</strong>.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"haematological\">Haematological</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Hypercoagulable:</strong> ↑ fibrinogen (often 4–6 g/L at term) and factors VII, VIII, X and vWF; ↓ protein S; reduced fibrinolysis → <strong>VTE risk</strong> (a leading direct cause of maternal death).</li><li>Fibrinogen below about 2 g/L in obstetric haemorrhage predicts severe bleeding.</li><li>Mild gestational thrombocytopenia is common.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"gastrointestinal\">Gastrointestinal</h2>\n<ul><li>Progesterone lowers <strong>LOS tone</strong>; the gravid uterus raises intragastric pressure and displaces the stomach. Gastric emptying is normal in pregnancy but <strong>delayed in labour, especially with opioids</strong>. Hence antacid prophylaxis and RSI for general anaesthesia from about the second trimester onwards (practice varies).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"other-systems\">Other systems</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Renal:</strong> renal plasma flow and <strong>GFR ↑ ~50%</strong> → normal pregnant creatinine is lower (a \"normal\" non-pregnant creatinine may indicate renal impairment).</li><li><strong>CNS:</strong> <strong>MAC falls by up to ~30–40%</strong> (progesterone, endorphins); local anaesthetic requirement for neuraxial block is reduced (engorged epidural veins reduce the CSF volume, hormonal effects).</li><li><strong>Plasma cholinesterase</strong> falls by about 25% (little clinical effect on suxamethonium).</li><li><strong>Endocrine:</strong> insulin resistance (gestational diabetes), raised thyroid-binding globulin.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> percentage changes; blood gas values in pregnancy; coagulation changes; MAC.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> explain why you would use a smaller tube, head-up pre-oxygenation and left uterine displacement for a general anaesthetic for category 1 caesarean section.</li></ul></div>","renal-gfr":"<h2 id=\"renal-blood-flow\">Renal blood flow</h2>\n<ul><li>About <strong>1.2 L/min (20–25% of cardiac output)</strong>; plasma flow about 650 mL/min.</li><li><strong>Autoregulation</strong> keeps flow and GFR fairly constant between a MAP of about <strong>75–160 mmHg</strong>, by:<ul><li>the <strong>myogenic response</strong> of the afferent arteriole (stretch → constriction);</li><li><strong>tubuloglomerular feedback</strong>: the <strong>macula densa</strong> senses NaCl delivery to the distal tubule; increased delivery → afferent vasoconstriction (adenosine) and less renin.</li></ul></li><li>Sympathetic stimulation, catecholamines and angiotensin II reduce flow. Angiotensin II constricts the <strong>efferent</strong> arteriole preferentially, supporting GFR when perfusion is low; this is why ACE inhibitors and ARBs can precipitate AKI in hypovolaemia or renal artery stenosis. NSAIDs remove prostaglandin-mediated afferent dilatation.</li><li>The <strong>outer cortex</strong> is well perfused; the <strong>medulla</strong> receives about 10% of flow and runs a low PO<sub>2</sub> (countercurrent exchange in the vasa recta), making it vulnerable to hypoxic injury.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"glomerular-filtration\">Glomerular filtration</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>GFR ≈ 125 mL/min (180 L/day)</strong> in a young adult; <strong>filtration fraction</strong> (GFR / renal plasma flow) ≈ <strong>20%</strong>.</li><li>Starling forces across the glomerular capillary: <strong>GFR = Kf [(P<sub>GC</sub> − P<sub>BS</sub>) − (π<sub>GC</sub> − π<sub>BS</sub>)]</strong>. Glomerular capillary pressure is high (about 55 mmHg) because the efferent arteriole provides downstream resistance.</li><li>The filtration barrier (fenestrated endothelium, negatively charged basement membrane, podocyte slit diaphragms) excludes molecules above about 70 kDa; albumin (~69 kDa, negatively charged) is largely retained.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"clearance\">Clearance</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Clearance = (U × V) / P</strong>: the volume of plasma completely cleared of a substance per minute.</li><li><strong>Inulin</strong> is freely filtered, neither reabsorbed nor secreted: its clearance = GFR (the gold standard).</li><li><strong>Creatinine</strong> clearance slightly <strong>overestimates</strong> GFR because a little creatinine is secreted.</li><li><strong>Para-aminohippuric acid (PAH)</strong> is filtered and almost completely secreted in one pass: its clearance ≈ effective renal plasma flow.</li><li><strong>eGFR</strong> (e.g. CKD-EPI) estimates GFR from plasma creatinine, age and sex; it is unreliable when creatinine is not at steady state (AKI) or muscle mass is abnormal. <strong>Cockcroft–Gault</strong> estimates creatinine clearance and is still used for some drug dosing.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"urea-and-creatinine\">Urea and creatinine</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Creatinine</strong> comes from muscle creatine at a fairly constant rate; plasma level rises only after GFR has fallen substantially and depends on muscle mass.</li><li><strong>Urea</strong> is filtered and partly reabsorbed (more when flow is low, so a high urea:creatinine ratio suggests hypovolaemia); it also rises with protein load, GI bleeding, catabolism and steroids.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"acute-kidney-injury\">Acute kidney injury</h2>\n<ul><li>Defined (KDIGO, as adopted by NICE) by a rise in creatinine of ≥ 26 µmol/L within 48 h or ≥ 1.5 × baseline within 7 days, or urine output &lt; 0.5 mL/kg/h for more than 6 hours.</li><li>Perioperative risk factors: CKD, age, diabetes, heart failure, sepsis, hypovolaemia and hypotension, nephrotoxins (NSAIDs, aminoglycosides, contrast), major and emergency surgery.</li><li>Prevention: maintain perfusion and volume, avoid nephrotoxins, review ACE inhibitors and diuretics, monitor urine output and creatinine.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> clearance calculations; why creatinine overestimates GFR; autoregulation mechanisms; drugs that impair autoregulation.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> an oliguric patient in recovery: assess volume status, catheter, perfusion and drugs, and explain your plan.</li></ul></div>","renal-tubule":"<h2 id=\"the-nephron-segment-by-segment\">The nephron segment by segment</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Segment</th><th>Key functions</th><th>Transporters / notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Proximal convoluted tubule</strong></td><td>Reabsorbs <strong>~65%</strong> of filtered Na<sup>+</sup> and water (isotonically), nearly all glucose and amino acids, ~80% of HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup>; secretes organic acids and bases (many drugs)</td><td>Na<sup>+</sup>/H<sup>+</sup> exchanger; carbonic anhydrase (acetazolamide acts here); SGLT2 (glucose; SGLT2 inhibitors)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Thin descending limb</strong></td><td>Permeable to water, not solutes</td><td>Water leaves into the hypertonic medulla</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Thick ascending limb</strong></td><td>Reabsorbs <strong>~25%</strong> of Na<sup>+</sup>; <strong>impermeable to water</strong> (\"diluting segment\")</td><td><strong>Na<sup>+</sup>/K<sup>+</sup>/2Cl<sup>−</sup> co-transporter</strong> (loop diuretics)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Distal convoluted tubule</strong></td><td>~5–8% of Na<sup>+</sup>; Ca<sup>2+</sup> reabsorption (PTH)</td><td><strong>Na<sup>+</sup>/Cl<sup>−</sup> co-transporter</strong> (thiazides)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Collecting duct</strong></td><td>Fine control: Na<sup>+</sup> reabsorption and K<sup>+</sup>/H<sup>+</sup> secretion (<strong>aldosterone</strong>, principal and intercalated cells); water reabsorption (<strong>ADH</strong>)</td><td>ENaC (amiloride); mineralocorticoid receptor (spironolactone); aquaporin-2</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"concentrating-urine-the-countercurrent-multiplier\">Concentrating urine: the countercurrent multiplier</h2>\n<ul><li>The thick ascending limb pumps NaCl into the interstitium without water, creating a gradient that increases towards the papilla (to about <strong>1200 mOsm/kg</strong>). <strong>Urea</strong> recycling from the medullary collecting duct adds to it; the <strong>vasa recta</strong> act as countercurrent exchangers that preserve the gradient.</li><li>With <strong>ADH</strong>, aquaporin-2 channels are inserted into the collecting duct, water follows the gradient, and urine can be concentrated to about 1200 mOsm/kg; without ADH it can be diluted to about 50 mOsm/kg.</li><li>Minimum obligatory urine volume ≈ solute load / maximal concentration (about 600 mOsm/day ÷ 1200 ≈ <strong>0.5 L/day</strong>).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"water-balance-adh-vasopressin\">Water balance: ADH (vasopressin)</h2>\n<ul><li>Made in the <strong>supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei</strong>, released from the <strong>posterior pituitary</strong>.</li><li>Released for <strong>raised plasma osmolality</strong> (osmoreceptors respond to changes of 1–2%) and for <strong>hypovolaemia/hypotension</strong> (less sensitive, but can override osmolality when large). Also by pain, nausea, surgery (stress), opioids, positive pressure ventilation.</li><li>V<sub>2</sub> receptors (collecting duct, cAMP → aquaporin-2): water retention. V<sub>1</sub> receptors: vasoconstriction.</li><li><strong>SIADH</strong> (surgery, pneumonia, intracranial pathology, drugs) → hyponatraemia with inappropriately concentrated urine. <strong>Diabetes insipidus</strong> (pituitary surgery, head injury, brainstem death) → dilute polyuria and hypernatraemia.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"sodium-balance\">Sodium balance</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Aldosterone</strong> (adrenal zona glomerulosa; stimulated by angiotensin II and <strong>raised K<sup>+</sup></strong>): increases Na<sup>+</sup> reabsorption and K<sup>+</sup> and H<sup>+</sup> secretion in the collecting duct.</li><li><strong>ANP/BNP</strong> (atrial stretch): natriuresis and vasodilation.</li><li>The sympathetic system and angiotensin II also increase proximal Na<sup>+</sup> reabsorption.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"potassium\">Potassium</h2>\n<p>Most filtered K<sup>+</sup> is reabsorbed proximally; urinary K<sup>+</sup> is determined by <strong>distal secretion</strong>, increased by aldosterone, high distal Na<sup>+</sup> delivery and flow (loop and thiazide diuretics → hypokalaemia) and alkalosis.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> where each diuretic acts; the countercurrent multiplier; stimuli for ADH and aldosterone; SIADH versus DI.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> a confused patient with a sodium of 121 mmol/L after surgery: assess volume and osmolality, and explain the likely cause and safe correction.</li></ul></div>","resp-altitude":"<h2 id=\"altitude\">Altitude</h2>\n<ul><li>Barometric pressure falls roughly exponentially with height: about <strong>half at 5,500 m</strong>. FiO<sub>2</sub> remains 0.21, but inspired PO<sub>2</sub> falls.</li><li><strong>Acute response:</strong> hypoxic stimulation of the carotid bodies → hyperventilation → respiratory alkalosis, which initially limits the ventilatory response.</li><li><strong>Acclimatisation (days to weeks):</strong> renal bicarbonate excretion corrects the alkalosis and ventilation rises further; <strong>2,3-DPG</strong> increases (right shift); <strong>erythropoietin</strong> raises haemoglobin over weeks; capillary density and mitochondrial changes.</li><li><strong>Illness:</strong> acute mountain sickness, high-altitude cerebral oedema and high-altitude pulmonary oedema (uneven hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction).</li><li><strong>Vaporisers at altitude:</strong> a variable-bypass vaporiser delivers roughly the same <strong>partial pressure</strong> of agent regardless of altitude, because saturated vapour pressure depends only on temperature. Since anaesthetic effect depends on partial pressure, the dial setting gives a similar clinical effect, although the percentage delivered is higher.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"diving-and-increased-pressure\">Diving and increased pressure</h2>\n<ul><li>Every <strong>10 m of seawater adds about 1 atmosphere</strong> (≈ 100 kPa).</li><li><strong>Boyle's law:</strong> gas volumes in closed spaces shrink on descent and expand on ascent → barotrauma of ears, sinuses and lungs; arterial gas embolism if a diver ascends holding their breath.</li><li><strong>Henry's law:</strong> more nitrogen dissolves at depth; if ascent is too fast it comes out of solution → <strong>decompression sickness</strong>.</li><li><strong>Nitrogen narcosis</strong> (below about 30 m) and <strong>oxygen toxicity</strong> (CNS toxicity with seizures at high PO<sub>2</sub>; pulmonary toxicity with prolonged exposure).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"hyperbaric-oxygen\">Hyperbaric oxygen</h2>\n<ul><li>Breathing 100% O<sub>2</sub> at 2–3 atmospheres raises dissolved oxygen enough to supply resting needs without haemoglobin (at about 3 atm absolute, dissolved O<sub>2</sub> is roughly 6 mL/dL).</li><li>Uses include decompression sickness, arterial gas embolism, severe carbon monoxide poisoning (shortens COHb half-life), necrotising soft-tissue infections and radiation injury.</li><li>Anaesthetic issues: monitoring and ventilator function under pressure, air-filled cuffs (fill with saline), pneumothorax must be drained, limited access.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> calculate inspired PO<sub>2</sub> at altitude; vaporiser performance at altitude; gas laws applied to diving.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> planning the transfer of a patient with a pneumothorax by air.</li></ul></div>","resp-co2-transport":"<h2 id=\"how-co-2-is-carried-in-arterial-blood\">How CO<sub>2</sub> is carried in arterial blood</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>Share of total (approx.)</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Bicarbonate</strong></td><td>~90%</td><td>Formed in red cells by <strong>carbonic anhydrase</strong>: CO<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O ⇌ H<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub> ⇌ H<sup>+</sup> + HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Carbamino compounds</strong></td><td>~5%</td><td>CO<sub>2</sub> bound to terminal amine groups, mostly on haemoglobin</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Dissolved</strong></td><td>~5%</td><td>CO<sub>2</sub> is about 20 times more soluble than O<sub>2</sub></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The <em>arteriovenous difference</em> in CO<sub>2</sub> content is carried in different proportions: carbamino compounds account for a much larger share (around a third) of CO<sub>2</sub> unloaded in the lungs than of the total.</p>\n<h2 id=\"chloride-hamburger-shift\">Chloride (Hamburger) shift</h2>\n<p>HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> formed in red cells diffuses out into plasma in exchange for Cl<sup>−</sup> (via band 3 protein). Venous red cells therefore contain more chloride, and they swell slightly because of the extra osmotically active particles.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-haldane-effect\">The Haldane effect</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Deoxygenated haemoglobin carries more CO<sub>2</sub></strong> than oxyhaemoglobin at the same PCO<sub>2</sub>. It is a better buffer for H<sup>+</sup> (a weaker acid) and forms carbamino compounds more readily.</li><li>In the tissues, oxygen unloading helps load CO<sub>2</sub>; in the lungs, oxygenation helps release CO<sub>2</sub>.</li><li>This is why venous pH is only slightly lower than arterial pH despite the added CO<sub>2</sub> (\"isohydric\" transport).</li><li>Clinical relevance: giving high-concentration oxygen to some patients with chronic CO<sub>2</sub> retention can raise PaCO<sub>2</sub>, partly through the Haldane effect and partly through worsening V/Q mismatch (release of hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction), rather than solely through loss of \"hypoxic drive\".</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-bohr-effect-for-contrast\">The Bohr effect (for contrast)</h2>\n<p>The <strong>Bohr effect</strong> is the effect of CO<sub>2</sub> and H<sup>+</sup> on <strong>oxygen</strong> binding (a right shift of the O<sub>2</sub> curve). The <strong>Haldane effect</strong> is the effect of <strong>oxygen</strong> on CO<sub>2</sub> carriage.</p>\n<h2 id=\"co-2-dissociation-curve\">CO<sub>2</sub> dissociation curve</h2>\n<p>Nearly <strong>linear</strong> over the physiological range and much steeper than the O<sub>2</sub> curve. So hyperventilating healthy lung units can offload extra CO<sub>2</sub> to compensate for poorly ventilated units, whereas they cannot add extra oxygen. This is why V/Q mismatch causes hypoxaemia well before hypercapnia.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> the proportions; chloride shift; Haldane versus Bohr; the shape of the CO<sub>2</sub> curve.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> interpreting a raised PaCO<sub>2</sub> in a patient with COPD given high-flow oxygen, and targeting saturations of 88–92% as in the BTS guideline.</li></ul></div>","resp-control":"<h2 id=\"the-controller\">The controller</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Respiratory centres in the medulla</strong> (dorsal and ventral respiratory groups; the pre-Bötzinger complex generates rhythm), modulated by the <strong>pons</strong> (pneumotaxic and apneustic centres) and by the cortex (voluntary control).</li><li><strong>Effectors:</strong> diaphragm (phrenic nerve, C3–5), intercostals, accessory muscles, upper airway dilators.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"sensors\">Sensors</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Central chemoreceptors</th><th>Peripheral chemoreceptors</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Site</td><td>Ventrolateral surface of the medulla</td><td><strong>Carotid bodies</strong> (main; glossopharyngeal nerve) and aortic bodies (vagus)</td></tr><tr><td>Stimulus</td><td><strong>H<sup>+</sup> in CSF</strong>, generated from CO<sub>2</sub> diffusing across the blood–brain barrier</td><td><strong>Falling PaO<sub>2</sub></strong> (steeply below about 8 kPa), rising PaCO<sub>2</sub> and H<sup>+</sup></td></tr><tr><td>Share of CO<sub>2</sub> response</td><td>~80%</td><td>~20%, but faster</td></tr><tr><td>Adaptation</td><td>CSF bicarbonate is adjusted over 24–48 h, resetting the response in chronic hypercapnia</td><td>Rapid</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Other inputs: stretch receptors (Hering–Breuer reflex, weak in adult humans), irritant and J receptors, joint and muscle receptors during exercise.</p>\n<h2 id=\"ventilatory-responses\">Ventilatory responses</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>CO<sub>2</sub> response:</strong> ventilation rises roughly linearly with PaCO<sub>2</sub> (about 2–3 L/min per mmHg, or 15–20 L/min per kPa, in healthy adults). Hypoxia steepens the slope.</li><li><strong>Hypoxic response:</strong> little change until PaO<sub>2</sub> falls below about 8 kPa, then a steep rise. The carotid bodies respond to <strong>PaO<sub>2</sub>, not oxygen content</strong>, so anaemia and CO poisoning do not stimulate them.</li><li><strong>Apnoeic threshold:</strong> the PaCO<sub>2</sub> below which spontaneous breathing stops. After hyperventilation under anaesthesia, breathing will not resume until PaCO<sub>2</sub> rises above it.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"effects-of-drugs\">Effects of drugs</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Opioids</strong> shift the CO<sub>2</sub> response curve to the right and flatten it, and reduce respiratory rate more than tidal volume.</li><li><strong>Volatile agents and propofol</strong> depress the CO<sub>2</sub> response, and the hypoxic response is depressed even at sub-anaesthetic concentrations (around 0.1 MAC). This matters in recovery.</li><li><strong>Benzodiazepines</strong> combined with opioids are synergistic.</li><li><strong>Doxapram</strong> stimulates peripheral (and at higher doses central) chemoreceptors.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> CO<sub>2</sub> response curves (draw them with the effects of opioids and hypoxia); the carotid body's stimulus; the apnoeic threshold.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> the drowsy patient in recovery with a respiratory rate of 6 after morphine: assess, support, and consider naloxone, with the physiology behind it.</li></ul></div>","resp-ippv":"<h2 id=\"respiratory-effects\">Respiratory effects</h2>\n<ul><li>Normal breathing is by <strong>negative</strong> intrapleural pressure; IPPV reverses this, pushing gas in with positive airway pressure.</li><li><strong>V/Q:</strong> ventilation goes preferentially to non-dependent, more compliant lung, while perfusion stays dependent → increased V/Q mismatch and <strong>dead space</strong>. Zone 1 may appear.</li><li><strong>Atelectasis</strong> in dependent regions (reduced FRC, absorption with high FiO<sub>2</sub>); PEEP and recruitment help.</li><li><strong>Ventilator-induced lung injury:</strong> volutrauma (overdistension), barotrauma (pneumothorax), atelectrauma (cyclical opening and closing), biotrauma (inflammatory mediators). Protective ventilation: tidal volume <strong>~6 mL/kg predicted body weight</strong> in ARDS (6–8 mL/kg in theatre), plateau pressure under 30 cmH<sub>2</sub>O, and appropriate PEEP.</li><li>Loss of mucociliary clearance, and risk of ventilator-associated pneumonia when prolonged.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"cardiovascular-effects\">Cardiovascular effects</h2>\n<ul><li>Raised intrathoracic pressure <strong>reduces venous return</strong> and right ventricular preload → lower cardiac output and blood pressure, especially if hypovolaemic (the basis of pulse pressure variation as a marker of fluid responsiveness).</li><li><strong>Increases RV afterload</strong> at high lung volumes (compression of alveolar vessels).</li><li><strong>Reduces LV afterload</strong> (lower transmural pressure) — helpful in LV failure, which is one reason CPAP helps acute cardiogenic pulmonary oedema.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"other-organs\">Other organs</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Renal:</strong> reduced renal blood flow and GFR, raised ADH and renin–angiotensin–aldosterone activity → fluid retention.</li><li><strong>Hepatic and splanchnic:</strong> reduced blood flow.</li><li><strong>Cerebral:</strong> raised intrathoracic pressure impedes cerebral venous drainage and can raise ICP; hyperventilation lowers PaCO<sub>2</sub> and cerebral blood flow.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> the cardiovascular effects of PEEP; causes of dead space during IPPV; protective ventilation targets.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> hypotension immediately after intubation in a septic, hypovolaemic patient: explain the physiology and your immediate management.</li></ul></div>","resp-lung-volumes":"<h2 id=\"key-facts\">Key facts</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Volume or capacity</th><th>Typical adult value (70 kg)</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Tidal volume (VT)</td><td>500 mL (about 7 mL/kg)</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Inspiratory reserve volume</td><td>~3 L</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Expiratory reserve volume (ERV)</td><td>~1.2 L</td><td>Falls when supine, with obesity and pregnancy</td></tr><tr><td>Residual volume (RV)</td><td>~1.2 L</td><td>Cannot be measured by spirometry</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Functional residual capacity (FRC)</strong> = ERV + RV</td><td><strong>~2.5 L upright</strong>, falls by roughly 0.5–1 L supine</td><td>The lungs' oxygen reserve during apnoea</td></tr><tr><td>Vital capacity</td><td>~4.5 L (≈ 60–70 mL/kg)</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Total lung capacity</td><td>~6 L</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li><strong>FRC</strong> is the volume at the end of a normal expiration, where the inward recoil of the lung balances the outward recoil of the chest wall.</li><li><strong>RV, FRC and TLC</strong> cannot be measured by simple spirometry: use <strong>helium dilution</strong> (measures communicating gas only), <strong>nitrogen washout</strong>, or <strong>body plethysmography</strong> (measures all intrathoracic gas, including trapped gas).</li><li><strong>Closing capacity (CC)</strong> is the lung volume at which small airways in dependent zones begin to close. It <strong>rises with age</strong>; it equals FRC when supine at about 44 years and when upright at about 66 years. When CC exceeds FRC, airway closure occurs during normal breathing, causing V/Q mismatch and shunt.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"what-reduces-frc\">What reduces FRC</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Posture:</strong> supine (abdominal contents push the diaphragm up), Trendelenburg.</li><li><strong>General anaesthesia:</strong> loss of diaphragmatic and chest wall tone reduces FRC by around 15–20%, whichever technique is used; atelectasis forms within minutes, especially with high FiO<sub>2</sub>.</li><li><strong>Obesity, pregnancy (term), abdominal distension, pneumoperitoneum.</strong></li><li><strong>Restrictive lung disease.</strong></li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"clinical-relevance\">Clinical relevance</h2>\n<ul><li>Pre-oxygenation fills the FRC with oxygen (denitrogenation). An FRC of 2.5 L at FeO<sub>2</sub> 0.9 holds over 2 L of O<sub>2</sub>; at resting consumption of about 250 mL/min, this is why a healthy adult can tolerate several minutes of apnoea. Obese and pregnant patients desaturate much faster: smaller FRC, higher oxygen consumption.</li><li>Head-up positioning, CPAP/PEEP during pre-oxygenation and high-flow nasal oxygen help maintain FRC and oxygen reserves.</li><li>Atelectasis after induction is reduced by avoiding unnecessarily high FiO<sub>2</sub> once intubated, recruitment manoeuvres and PEEP.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> definitions; which volumes spirometry cannot measure; helium dilution versus plethysmography; effect of age and posture on closing capacity.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> \"Why did this patient with a BMI of 45 desaturate so quickly at induction, and what would you do differently?\" Link FRC, oxygen consumption and closing capacity to a practical plan.</li></ul></div>","resp-mechanics":"<h2 id=\"compliance\">Compliance</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Compliance = ΔV / ΔP.</strong> Normal total respiratory system compliance is about <strong>100 mL/cmH<sub>2</sub>O</strong> (lung ~200 and chest wall ~200 mL/cmH<sub>2</sub>O, which add as reciprocals: 1/C<sub>total</sub> = 1/C<sub>lung</sub> + 1/C~chest wall~).</li><li><strong>Static compliance</strong> is measured with no gas flow (plateau pressure), so it reflects elastic properties only. <strong>Dynamic compliance</strong> is measured during flow (peak pressure) and is lower because it includes airway resistance.</li><li>The <strong>pressure–volume curve</strong> of the lung is sigmoid: compliance is lowest at very low volumes (alveolar collapse) and near TLC (overdistension). Normal breathing at FRC sits on the steep part.</li><li>Compliance is reduced by pulmonary fibrosis, oedema, ARDS, atelectasis, obesity (chest wall), and increased in emphysema and old age (loss of elastic tissue).</li><li><strong>Hysteresis:</strong> the inflation and deflation limbs differ, because of surfactant and alveolar recruitment.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"surface-tension-and-surfactant\">Surface tension and surfactant</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Laplace's law</strong> for a sphere: <strong>P = 2T / r</strong>. Without surfactant, small alveoli would empty into large ones.</li><li><strong>Surfactant</strong> (dipalmitoyl phosphatidylcholine, from type II pneumocytes) lowers surface tension, more so as alveoli shrink. This stabilises alveoli, increases compliance, reduces the work of breathing and helps keep alveoli dry (less fluid is drawn in from capillaries).</li><li>Deficiency: neonatal respiratory distress syndrome (surfactant production matures from about 34 weeks' gestation).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"airway-resistance\">Airway resistance</h2>\n<ul><li>Laminar flow: resistance ∝ <strong>η·l / r<sup>4</sup></strong> (Hagen–Poiseuille). Halving the radius increases resistance 16-fold.</li><li>Most resistance in normal lungs is in the <strong>medium-sized bronchi</strong>, not the small airways, because small airways are so numerous that their total cross-sectional area is huge.</li><li>Resistance falls as lung volume increases (radial traction), so it rises at low lung volumes, with bronchospasm, secretions, and narrow or kinked tracheal tubes.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"work-of-breathing\">Work of breathing</h2>\n<ul><li>Work = pressure × volume. It has an <strong>elastic</strong> component (overcome compliance; greater with large tidal volumes) and a <strong>resistive</strong> component (overcome airway and tissue resistance; greater with high flow rates and fast breathing).</li><li>At rest breathing uses only about 1–2% of total oxygen consumption; this can rise dramatically in respiratory disease.</li><li>Patients naturally choose a respiratory rate that minimises work: stiff lungs → rapid shallow breathing; obstructed airways → slow deep breathing.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> calculating compliance from ventilator pressures; static versus dynamic compliance; Laplace; effect of radius on resistance.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> interpreting a rising peak pressure with a normal plateau pressure (resistance problem: kinked tube, bronchospasm, secretions) versus rising peak and plateau (compliance problem: endobronchial tube, pneumothorax, abdominal insufflation, light anaesthesia with chest wall rigidity).</li></ul></div>","resp-o2-transport":"<h2 id=\"oxygen-content-and-delivery\">Oxygen content and delivery</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Oxygen content:</strong> <strong>CaO<sub>2</sub> = (1.34 × Hb × SaO<sub>2</sub>) + (0.023 × PaO<sub>2</sub>)</strong>, with Hb in g/dL, PaO<sub>2</sub> in kPa and content in mL/dL. (The Hüfner constant is often quoted between 1.31 and 1.39.)</li><li>Normal CaO<sub>2</sub> ≈ 20 mL/dL; CvO<sub>2</sub> ≈ 15 mL/dL. Dissolved oxygen is small: about 0.3 mL/dL at a normal PaO<sub>2</sub>, but it matters in hyperbaric therapy.</li><li><strong>Delivery: DO<sub>2</sub> = CO × CaO<sub>2</sub></strong> ≈ 5 L/min × 200 mL/L ≈ <strong>1000 mL/min</strong>. <strong>Consumption VO<sub>2</sub></strong> ≈ <strong>250 mL/min</strong> at rest, so resting <strong>oxygen extraction ≈ 25%</strong> and mixed venous saturation ≈ 75%.</li><li>DO<sub>2</sub> falls with anaemia, hypoxaemia and low cardiac output. Of these, the body compensates first by increasing cardiac output and extraction.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-oxygen-cascade\">The oxygen cascade</h2>\n<p>Inspired (dry air) ≈ 21 kPa → humidified tracheal ≈ 19.8 kPa → alveolar ≈ 13.3 kPa → arterial ≈ 12–13 kPa (venous admixture) → capillary → mitochondria, where PO<sub>2</sub> may be only about 0.1–1 kPa.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-dissociation-curve\">The dissociation curve</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Sigmoid</strong> because of <strong>cooperative binding</strong>: binding of each O<sub>2</sub> increases the affinity of the remaining haem groups (T to R state).</li><li>Reference points: <strong>P50 ≈ 3.5 kPa</strong> (26.6 mmHg); venous point ≈ 5.3 kPa / 75%; arterial ≈ 13.3 kPa / 97–98%.</li><li>The flat upper part protects saturation despite falls in PaO<sub>2</sub>; the steep part allows large unloading for small falls in tissue PO<sub>2</sub>.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Right shift (↓ affinity, ↑ P50, better unloading)</th><th>Left shift (↑ affinity, ↓ P50)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>↑ H<sup>+</sup> (↓ pH), ↑ PCO<sub>2</sub> (Bohr effect)</td><td>Alkalosis, hypocapnia</td></tr><tr><td>↑ temperature</td><td>Hypothermia</td></tr><tr><td>↑ 2,3-DPG (chronic hypoxia, anaemia, altitude)</td><td>↓ 2,3-DPG (stored blood)</td></tr><tr><td>Pregnancy (maternal curve)</td><td>Fetal haemoglobin, carboxyhaemoglobin, methaemoglobin</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li><strong>Carbon monoxide</strong> binds haemoglobin with over 200 times the affinity of oxygen: it reduces content <strong>and</strong> shifts the curve left. Standard pulse oximetry reads COHb as oxyhaemoglobin.</li><li><strong>Myoglobin</strong> has a hyperbolic curve with a very low P50 (≈ 0.3 kPa); it stores oxygen in muscle.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> calculate content and delivery; the effect of anaemia versus hypoxaemia; causes of curve shifts; why stored blood has low 2,3-DPG.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> explain oxygen delivery to a relative or colleague in the context of a bleeding, anaemic patient; draw and annotate the curve.</li></ul></div>","resp-pulmonary-circulation":"<h2 id=\"pressures-and-resistance\">Pressures and resistance</h2>\n<ul><li>A low-pressure, high-flow, highly compliant circuit: PA pressure about <strong>25/10 mmHg (mean ~15)</strong>, left atrial pressure ~5–8 mmHg.</li><li><strong>PVR = (mean PAP − LAP) / CO × 80</strong> dyn·s·cm<sup>−5</sup>; normal about <strong>100–200 dyn·s·cm<sup>−5</sup></strong> (about one tenth of SVR).</li><li>PVR <strong>falls as cardiac output rises</strong>, by <strong>recruitment</strong> of closed capillaries and <strong>distension</strong> of open ones, so pressure rises little with exercise.</li><li>PVR is lowest at <strong>FRC</strong>: at low lung volumes extra-alveolar vessels narrow; at high lung volumes alveolar vessels are compressed.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"hypoxic-pulmonary-vasoconstriction-hpv\">Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction (HPV)</h2>\n<ul><li>Pulmonary arterioles constrict in response to <strong>low alveolar PO<sub>2</sub></strong> (and to a lesser extent low mixed venous PO<sub>2</sub>), diverting blood to better-ventilated lung and reducing shunt.</li><li>Mechanism: inhibition of oxygen-sensitive potassium channels in smooth muscle → depolarisation → calcium entry.</li><li>Enhanced by acidosis; inhibited by alkalosis, vasodilators (nitroprusside, GTN), and (weakly, dose-dependently) by volatile anaesthetics. Largely preserved with propofol.</li><li>Relevant in one-lung ventilation; global HPV (altitude, chronic hypoxia) causes pulmonary hypertension.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"non-respiratory-functions\">Non-respiratory functions</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Filter:</strong> traps small emboli, thrombi and air bubbles.</li><li><strong>Reservoir:</strong> holds about 500 mL of blood, which can move into the systemic circulation.</li><li><strong>Metabolic:</strong> the endothelium converts <strong>angiotensin I to angiotensin II</strong> (ACE), inactivates <strong>bradykinin</strong>, and removes much of circulating <strong>serotonin</strong> and <strong>noradrenaline</strong> and some prostaglandins. Adrenaline, histamine, isoprenaline and angiotensin II pass through largely unchanged.</li><li><strong>Drug handling:</strong> first-pass uptake of basic lipophilic drugs such as fentanyl and lidocaine.</li><li><strong>Defence:</strong> mucociliary clearance, alveolar macrophages, IgA.</li><li>Surfactant production; heat and water loss; acid–base regulation via CO<sub>2</sub>.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> calculate PVR; which substances are metabolised in the lung; factors affecting HPV.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> explaining the management of hypoxaemia during one-lung ventilation, including why HPV matters.</li></ul></div>","resp-vq":"<h2 id=\"distribution-of-ventilation-and-perfusion\">Distribution of ventilation and perfusion</h2>\n<ul><li>In the upright lung <strong>both</strong> ventilation and perfusion increase towards the base, but perfusion increases more steeply. So V/Q is <strong>high at the apex (~3)</strong> and <strong>low at the base (~0.6)</strong>; overall about <strong>0.8</strong> (alveolar ventilation ~4 L/min, cardiac output ~5 L/min).</li><li><strong>West zones:</strong> zone 1 (P<sub>A</sub> &gt; P<sub>a</sub> &gt; P<sub>v</sub>) has no flow: alveolar dead space, which appears only if alveolar pressure is raised (IPPV) or pulmonary pressure falls (hypovolaemia). Zone 2 (P<sub>a</sub> &gt; P<sub>A</sub> &gt; P<sub>v</sub>): flow depends on the arterial–alveolar difference. Zone 3 (P<sub>a</sub> &gt; P<sub>v</sub> &gt; P<sub>A</sub>): continuous flow.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"causes-of-hypoxaemia\">Causes of hypoxaemia</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Cause</th><th>A–a gradient</th><th>Response to raising FiO<sub>2</sub></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Low inspired PO<sub>2</sub> (altitude, hypoxic gas mixture)</td><td>Normal</td><td>Corrects</td></tr><tr><td>Hypoventilation</td><td>Normal</td><td>Corrects (but CO<sub>2</sub> still rises)</td></tr><tr><td>V/Q mismatch (low V/Q)</td><td>Raised</td><td>Improves</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Shunt</strong> (V/Q = 0)</td><td>Raised</td><td><strong>Little improvement</strong> once shunt exceeds ~30%</td></tr><tr><td>Diffusion impairment</td><td>Raised</td><td>Improves</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li>The <strong>alveolar gas equation</strong>: <strong>P<sub>A</sub>O<sub>2</sub> = F<sub>I</sub>O<sub>2</sub> (P<sub>B</sub> − P<sub>H2O</sub>) − P<sub>a</sub>CO<sub>2</sub> / RQ</strong>. Breathing air at sea level: 0.21 × (101 − 6.3) − 5.3/0.8 ≈ 13.3 kPa.</li><li>A normal A–a gradient is under about 2 kPa in young adults, rising with age.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"shunt\">Shunt</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Shunt equation:</strong> <strong>Qs/Qt = (CcO<sub>2</sub> − CaO<sub>2</sub>) / (CcO<sub>2</sub> − CvO<sub>2</sub>)</strong>, where Cc is end-capillary content (calculated from P<sub>A</sub>O<sub>2</sub>).</li><li>Normal physiological shunt is 2–5% (bronchial and Thebesian veins). Pathological shunt: atelectasis, pneumonia, pulmonary oedema, ARDS, intracardiac right-to-left shunt, one-lung ventilation.</li><li>Oxygen fails to correct shunt because shunted blood never meets the enriched gas, and blood from well-ventilated units is already nearly saturated, so it cannot carry much extra.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"dead-space\">Dead space</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Physiological dead space = anatomical + alveolar.</strong> Anatomical about <strong>2 mL/kg</strong> (~150 mL).</li><li><strong>Bohr equation:</strong> <strong>V<sub>D</sub>/V<sub>T</sub> = (P<sub>a</sub>CO<sub>2</sub> − P<sub>E</sub>CO<sub>2</sub>) / P<sub>a</sub>CO<sub>2</sub></strong> (using mixed expired CO<sub>2</sub>). Normal V<sub>D</sub>/V<sub>T</sub> is 0.2–0.35.</li><li>Anatomical dead space is measured by <strong>Fowler's method</strong> (single-breath nitrogen washout).</li><li>Alveolar dead space increases with pulmonary embolism, low cardiac output and hypovolaemia, and high airway pressures, and widens the P<sub>a</sub>CO<sub>2</sub>–E<sub>T</sub>CO<sub>2</sub> gap.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> alveolar gas equation calculations; the effect of FiO<sub>2</sub> on shunt versus V/Q mismatch; Bohr and Fowler.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> a desaturating patient in recovery: work through the causes systematically (FiO<sub>2</sub>, ventilation, V/Q and shunt, diffusion, low mixed venous content) and act.</li></ul></div>","thermoregulation":"<h2 id=\"normal-regulation\">Normal regulation</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Core temperature</strong> is about 37°C (range about 36.5–37.5°C), with a circadian variation. The <strong>hypothalamus</strong> integrates input from peripheral and central thermoreceptors (cold via Aδ, warmth via C fibres).</li><li>The <strong>interthreshold range</strong> (between the thresholds for sweating and vasoconstriction) is normally only about <strong>0.2–0.4°C</strong>.</li><li><strong>Heat loss responses:</strong> cutaneous vasodilation, sweating. <strong>Heat conservation and gain:</strong> vasoconstriction, behavioural changes, <strong>shivering</strong> (can double or triple heat production and oxygen consumption), and <strong>non-shivering thermogenesis</strong> in <strong>brown fat</strong> (important in neonates; uncoupling protein, stimulated by noradrenaline).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"how-anaesthesia-causes-hypothermia\">How anaesthesia causes hypothermia</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Redistribution (first hour):</strong> anaesthetic-induced vasodilation lets heat flow from the core to the cooler periphery. Core temperature typically falls by <strong>about 1–1.5°C in the first hour</strong>. Pre-warming reduces this.</li><li><strong>Linear phase (2–3 hours):</strong> heat loss exceeds metabolic heat production (reduced by about 20–30% under anaesthesia).</li><li><strong>Plateau:</strong> core temperature stabilises when vasoconstriction returns at a lower threshold (not with neuraxial block, which prevents it in the blocked area).</li><li>General anaesthesia <strong>widens the interthreshold range to about 2–4°C</strong> (the vasoconstriction threshold falls, the sweating threshold rises).</li><li><strong>Mechanisms of heat loss:</strong> <strong>radiation</strong> (the largest, about 40%), <strong>convection</strong> (~30%), <strong>evaporation</strong> (~15%, more with open body cavities and respiratory tract), <strong>conduction</strong> (~5%).</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"consequences-of-hypothermia-core-36-c\">Consequences of hypothermia (core &lt; 36°C)</h2>\n<p>Increased surgical site infection, coagulopathy and blood loss, prolonged drug action (e.g. neuromuscular blockers), shivering and increased oxygen consumption, myocardial ischaemia, patient discomfort, longer recovery.</p>\n<h2 id=\"prevention-nice-cg65\">Prevention (NICE CG65)</h2>\n<ul><li>Measure temperature before induction, every 30 minutes during anaesthesia, and in recovery. Do not start anaesthesia (unless urgent) if the core temperature is below 36°C.</li><li><strong>Forced-air warming</strong> for procedures over 30 minutes (or at higher risk); warm IV fluids and blood above 500 mL; warm irrigation fluids; theatre temperature at least 21°C while the patient is exposed.</li><li>Neonates and infants lose heat especially fast (large surface area to volume ratio, thin skin, little subcutaneous fat).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> the three phases; mechanisms of heat loss and their proportions; brown fat.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> planning temperature management for a long laparotomy in an elderly patient, or explaining shivering in recovery.</li></ul></div>"};
